WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:11.110
Alright, so I was thinking today about how
to explain this a little bit further.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:23.310
So let’s say you’re going for a job interview.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.240
And you’re nervous about it.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:34.100
And you go into the job interview and there
are some people there, and some chairs and

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.640
tables, and a room that’s like this, maybe
not quite this ugly, but something like this.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.059
What’s in the room?

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:47.079
You’re nervous.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:51.070
You’ve seen chairs and tables before, you’ve
seen people.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:56.809
What’s in the room?

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:01.010
How about a set of alternate futures?

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.620
Right?

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.050
You know, that isn’t how we think.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.640
It’s how we act.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.720
And you know, if you talk about it, I was
nervous about going to this job interview.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.720
Why?

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.380
Well I thought I might be evaluated.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.550
Okay, well why does that matter?

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.970
Well, we know partly why it matters if we
look at this figure again.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:28.200
I’m pretty happy with this figure, all things
considered.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.680
You know, and you might say, well if I…
maybe it’ll shape my faith and my belief

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.950
that I’m a good person.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.640
That would be a little silly for an interview.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.360
So if you’re thinking that way I would say
you’re not thinking specifically enough.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.200
Because there’s lots of reasons you might
not do well at the job interview, including

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.140
the fact that they might have already given
the job to someone else, for all you know.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:03.750
So you don’t want to leap to the top of
the hierarchy without cause.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.149
So one of the things you might say is that
in the room there, there are threats that

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:17.870
might be posed to any level of the hierarchy
of beliefs and behaviours that you use to

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.780
interact with the world.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.032
Now the terror management people would tell
you… let’s assume that this [points to

00:02:25.032 --> 00:02:26.249
figure on board] is a belief structure.

00:02:26.249 --> 00:02:30.689
I don’t care whether you agree with the
way it’s represented graphically or not,

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:34.930
for the time being, but let’s just assume
that what we’re talking about is a belief

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:35.930
structure.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:42.319
Now the terror management people would tell
you that you have that belief structure, in

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.950
large part to protect you from the fear of
death, from death anxiety.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:53.269
But there’s a bunch of problems with that
idea, not least that it actually side-steps

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:56.390
the issue of the function of the belief system.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:01.170
It’s like, you don’t exactly have a belief
system to make philosophical sense out of

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.790
the world.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:12.439
You have a system of tools, that are embodied,
that allow you to actually act in the world.

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:18.719
Now you might also have an articulated representation
of that on top that’s more or less accurate.

00:03:18.719 --> 00:03:22.209
Probably less, all things considered.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:26.079
And you know, maybe if your faith in that
is shaken, your faith in what you do will

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:29.650
also be shaken, because the two things are
associated.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:34.680
But we should always remember that a belief
system doesn’t just protect you from the

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.859
fear of death, it protects you from death.

00:03:37.859 --> 00:03:39.319
And that’s a lot different, you know.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:46.849
I mean, this whole thing is your set of skills,
and the perceptions and thoughts and emotions

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:51.300
that go along with those skills, and then
it’s an integrated structure consisting

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.269
of the particular manner in which you’ve
integrated all your own little sub-parts and

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.569
then in relationship to the whole community.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:03.810
You could also think about it, in some sense,
as a holographic representation of the culture.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.980
Now, I don’t know how much you guys know
about holographs.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.099
I don’t know very much about them either.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:15.279
But there are some things I know about them…
so if you shine a light on a holograph, you’ll

00:04:15.279 --> 00:04:21.949
get a 3D image of whatever was there when
the holograph was created; it’s created

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:24.930
by lasers.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.889
And if you take a holograph and you break
it into pieces, and then you shine a light

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:33.220
on the pieces, you get the object in each
piece.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.639
But it’s low resolution.

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:39.879
It’s a nice way of thinking about a person.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:45.729
Like if your brain is damaged you’ll be
there, but as a lower resolution representation

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:46.729
of yourself.

00:04:46.729 --> 00:04:49.530
But it’s also a really nice way of thinking
about what culture is.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:05:00.349
You know, culture is this massive structure
of multiple levels and domains, but it’s

00:05:00.349 --> 00:05:01.349
reflected in each of us.

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:03.460
Like, we’re each partial carriers of it.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:08.610
So we embody a partial representation of the
structure.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.940
I mean literally, we embody it.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.289
And then we also represent it abstractly.

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:18.740
And you know, part of the reason that you
go to a university is to, in some sense, improve

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.360
your capacity to articulate your structure.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.919
Right, because you’re a cultural creature…

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:25.650
it’s like, why are you the way you are?

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.879
Well, some of it is psychological, some of
it is sociological, some of it is historical.

00:05:29.879 --> 00:05:31.580
Like a lot of it is historical.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:37.550
So when you go and you study a social science
or a humanity, you’re studying your own

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:38.789
personality.

00:05:38.789 --> 00:05:48.560
Now, I wrote a paper a while back with a couple
of my graduate students, and I think it was

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.110
called The Entropy…

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.669
God, you’d think I would remember this…

00:05:54.669 --> 00:05:57.050
The Entropy… doesn’t matter.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:02.750
The hypothesis was, and this is something
I’ve been think about for a long time, that

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:07.400
the world is very, very complex, and so what
that means is that the act of getting from

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.810
one place to another is often very, very difficult.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.880
And part of what you’re doing when you put
together a functional sub-module is you’re

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.820
putting together a little routine that will
enable you to transform some part of time

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:23.990
and space from what it is into what you want
it to be.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:27.919
And in some sense what you’re doing is reducing
the complexity of that situation.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:30.930
So imagine this, you’re going for your job
interview.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.509
And you’re knocking ‘em dead.

00:06:33.509 --> 00:06:34.520
What happens?

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:42.229
Well, a whole bunch of possibilities, all
that set of possibilities that was concerning

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:46.730
you, starts to collapse towards a single solution.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:52.229
And that single solution is, if you’re doing
very well in the interview, it’s a positive

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:53.229
solution.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:56.560
So you’re anxiety disappears and it’s
replaced with positive emotion.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.060
You get enthusiastic and excited, because
what’s happening in that room is that the

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.030
path to a desirable future is opening.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.910
Now you can’t see any of this, you know,
because we’re so blinded by the idea that

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.219
we see objects…

00:07:10.219 --> 00:07:15.780
we fail to perceive, in any articulated sense,
what it is that we’re actually dealing with.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:21.110
And I think that one way of thinking about
it is that you’re always on the edge of

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.539
something that’s potential, and that’s
where you exist.

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:30.099
You exist on this moving boundary of potential.

00:07:30.099 --> 00:07:37.449
And you can interact with it such that you
form it and it passes by you into the past.

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:45.729
And hopefully you’re forming it in a way
that is in concordance with your better health

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:50.229
and perhaps with the better health of the
people around you, that would be optimal.

00:07:50.229 --> 00:07:54.159
So you’re realising the potential in a genuine,
non-metaphorical sense.

00:07:54.159 --> 00:07:58.740
And that’s the thing about these representations,
you know.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.110
They’re often described as mythical, or
mythological, or metaphorical.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.469
But you know, they’re really not metaphorical.

00:08:05.469 --> 00:08:11.509
Well, that’s not exactly right, because
the Great Mother, the Great Father, they’re

00:08:11.509 --> 00:08:17.720
partially metaphorical but the thing that
they stand for as metaphors is something other

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.849
than the things that we conceptualise when
we look at the world from an objective perspective.

00:08:21.849 --> 00:08:26.969
Now, we’ve talked a little bit about the
figure of the Dragon of Chaos, and I think

00:08:26.969 --> 00:08:32.560
that out of all the metaphorical images, it’s
the most difficult to grasp, because it’s

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.820
that which has not yet been encountered, so
it’s neither unknown nor known.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.029
And you think, well you can’t even describe
that.

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:45.230
Yeah, but you can encounter it, and you can
encounter it when you go into a situation

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:46.290
that’s radically new.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:51.871
Let’s say it’s just the second before
you walk into a high-stakes interview, you

00:08:51.871 --> 00:08:54.070
know, just before you open the door.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:58.920
I would say that’s as close as you get,
in a normal day, to a direct encounter with

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.860
the dragon of chaos, because you don’t know
what’s going to happen.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.870
And to say, well, you’re in a room and there’s
a door in front of you, you know it’s almost

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:07.820
moronic to think of it that way, right?

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:10.360
Because what the hell does that have to do
with anything?

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.459
It’s… what’s in there is unbelievably…

00:09:13.459 --> 00:09:19.530
like it’s the localisation of unbelievably
complex webs of causality.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.149
That’s what’s waiting for you inside that
room.

00:09:22.149 --> 00:09:27.089
And then you might say, well is it structured
in any real way, and the answer to that is

00:09:27.089 --> 00:09:28.089
not really.

00:09:28.089 --> 00:09:33.190
It’s no more structured than… it’s even
less structured than a book you’re reading.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:37.139
You know, if you’re reading a book you might
say, well where’s the meaning in this book?

00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:40.399
And the answer to that is, well it’s not
in the book.

00:09:40.399 --> 00:09:44.910
Like it’s partly there, because you need
the words and the sentences and the paragraphs

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:49.320
and all that, but you need the whole cultural
context around it in order to interpret it.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.160
So you’re not reading it blank.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.760
You come to it with a set of, a very large
set of presuppositions.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.350
Enough of them so that you can understand
the author.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:05.450
Now maybe you can’t, and the book’s over
your head, or it’s badly written, but typically,

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:11.339
you come at it with the machinery in place,
so that when you interact with the words and

00:10:11.339 --> 00:10:17.170
the phrases and the sentences and so forth,
revelations of new meaning pour forth.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:21.829
And so, I don’t know how to say it any clearer
than that, that’s what the meaning of the

00:10:21.829 --> 00:10:22.829
book is.

00:10:22.829 --> 00:10:26.279
And it’s in the dialectical interaction
between you and the book.

00:10:26.279 --> 00:10:31.060
Well, if you walk into an interview room,
it’s even less given, in some sense, than

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:32.060
the book, right.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:36.170
Because what’s going to happen in there,
which really means which of the many futures

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:41.660
that that room contains is going to manifest
itself, depends very much on how you act.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:48.410
So, it’s in that way that you collapse the
potential that’s in a particular place and

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:53.860
you extract something out of it that’s…
well, and that’s when it gets tricky…

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:56.100
neutral, positive, or negative?

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.760
Maybe you bomb the interview, right?

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.850
And then you might say, well what’s happened?

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:00.850
What happened?

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:04.300
Well that’s a very complicated question.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.890
Then you’re stuck with this hierarchy, right.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.190
And that’s what you’re going to do when
you go home.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:12.079
You’re going to say “well maybe I didn’t
dress properly, or maybe I didn’t get to

00:11:12.079 --> 00:11:15.720
the interview on time, or they asked me a
bunch of questions I wasn’t prepared for,

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:20.320
or Jesus, I didn’t know anything about what’s
going on, my whole education was a waste of

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:22.199
time, and what the hell am I doing”?

00:11:22.199 --> 00:11:24.889
And then you might go “I’m never going
to get a job”.

00:11:24.889 --> 00:11:29.790
And you know, you could say in some sense
that all of those hypotheses, as you move

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:37.510
up and down the hierarchy, you move from trivial
trouble to really not good trouble, as you

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:43.220
bounce, and all of that is an attempt to specify,
in some sense, what that room contained.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.970
And it’s not easy.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:51.460
It’s weird too, because the room can contain
things that alter your past.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:56.920
Because maybe you say, well maybe you’re
going for a computer science job, maybe you’re

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:57.920
not, whatever.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:01.150
You go in there and you think you’re the
top person in your class, and maybe you were,

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.260
but you know the people in there are so far
ahead of you that you can’t even imagine

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.260
ever catching up.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:11.279
And you know, when you come out of there,
the manner in which you construe your educational

00:12:11.279 --> 00:12:14.399
history is going to be radically transformed.

00:12:14.399 --> 00:12:18.339
So there’s a lot of trouble in that room.

00:12:18.339 --> 00:12:20.170
There’s a lot.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.160
Now one of the things we though in this paper…

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:28.790
you can think of each of those little eggs,
they’re really eggs, as a container, and

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.260
what they contain is entropy, trouble.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:35.470
And the smaller ones contain smaller amounts
of entropy and the bigger ones contain bigger

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:36.470
amounts of entropy.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:38.880
And so, here’s another example.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:44.080
You’re driving down the highway and your
car sputters and grinds to a halt.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.230
Maybe it makes a nice radical noise while
it’s doing it.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.130
So okay, well, what happened?

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:58.630
Well the answer is, the simple answer is,
your car malfunctioned and you had to pull

00:12:58.630 --> 00:12:59.630
off the road.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:00.630
That’s a really object-oriented analysis.

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:04.800
But what’s actually happened is that you
had a conceptualisation of the car as a pragmatic

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:11.279
tool, because what do you know about the damn
car?

00:13:11.279 --> 00:13:17.600
The car was an affordance, that’s the right
word, that would get you from point A to point

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:20.170
B, and you could have a very low-resolution
model of it, right?

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:24.680
It can just look like a plastic shell, which
is sort of what a car looks like.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:29.360
And as long as you see how low-resolution
that is, you could replace it with a cartoon

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.339
and you wouldn’t even notice.

00:13:32.339 --> 00:13:36.089
That’s good enough representation as long
as the thing is doing what it’s supposed

00:13:36.089 --> 00:13:37.089
to be doing.

00:13:37.089 --> 00:13:40.560
But as soon as it stops doing what it’s
supposed to be doing then it could be any

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.130
old thing.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:43.930
And you think, well what does any old thing
mean?

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:47.160
Well maybe you’re on a deserted highway
and it’s one o’clock in the morning.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.430
And you know, someone pulls over to help you.

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:51.470
Okay, well, what’s that?

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:56.290
Well, you know, believe me, your imagination
will rapidly span the world of possibility

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:00.290
from “oh good, someone’s come to help”
to “I’m going to be dragged off in the

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:01.560
bush and killed”.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.670
And that’s all contained in that sequence
of events.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:11.440
And then even if no one does stop, and you
have to worry about exactly what’s gone

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.880
wrong, and you have to worry about how you’re
going to fix it and you have to worry about

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:16.950
the mechanic that might help you, and you
have to concern yourself with whether or not

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.779
you’re going to need a new car, and then
you have to think about your finances.

00:14:19.779 --> 00:14:28.160
It’s like, the idea that that car is a bounded
entity, like it looks, is only true when it

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.160
works.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.029
And as soon as it doesn’t work, then God,
it’s any old thing.

00:14:33.029 --> 00:14:36.100
And that, in some sense, is the dragon of
chaos.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:40.670
Now, what seems to me to have happened…
because you might think, well how did people

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:44.480
come to conceptualise such a thing, because
I would think that, of all the things that

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:50.310
are difficult to conceptualise, the category
of all things that you have not yet encountered

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.839
is the most difficult thing to discover, right?

00:14:52.839 --> 00:14:53.839
It’s kind of like zero.

00:14:53.839 --> 00:14:57.550
It took people a long time to figure out zero,
and no wonder.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:00.010
We need a symbol for nothing.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.470
Well, this is even worse.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.149
We need a representation for potential.

00:15:05.149 --> 00:15:06.610
Well, you think, why?

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:11.290
Well it’s because it’s actually potential
that you’re interacting with all the time.

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:17.569
Now what seems to me to have happened was
that… you know, this is an evolutionary

00:15:17.569 --> 00:15:23.730
just-so story, so I’m not assuming that
it worked precisely this way, but it doesn’t

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:24.730
matter.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.290
Because I’m going to touch on the appropriate
features.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.940
Well one of the things we know about social
animals, generally speaking, and animals in

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:34.769
general, is that they have a territory, right?

00:15:34.769 --> 00:15:37.829
And so you think, well what is their territory
exactly?

00:15:37.829 --> 00:15:40.330
And you might say, well you can mark it out.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:46.410
Here’s a… oh, what would have a territory?

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.230
Like a tiger, they’re pretty solitary, so
it’ll be simple.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.949
They have a big territory, right?

00:15:51.949 --> 00:15:54.950
And it’s hundreds of square miles.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:57.899
And the reason for that is, well they’re
tigers.

00:15:57.899 --> 00:16:01.740
You can’t have like fifty of them, because
they’ll just eat everything that’s there.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:06.630
You know, you have to have a big territory
to support one apex predator.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.560
So then you might say, well what exactly is
the territory of the tiger?

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:13.000
And you’d say well here are its boundaries.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.050
This is where it roams.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:18.690
But that’s not the right way of thinking
about the territory of the tiger.

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:24.930
Because the territory of the tiger is actually
all the places where, when the tiger does

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:29.319
its thing, the things that the tiger wants
or expects, happen.

00:16:29.319 --> 00:16:30.730
That’s the tiger’s territory.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.709
It’s the same with you.

00:16:32.709 --> 00:16:36.650
You’re at home wherever you know what to
do.

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:42.970
And what you’re doing when you know what
to do is you know how to match this hierarchy,

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:47.240
which is partly action-oriented, you know
how to match the hierarchy with the situation.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:53.819
So that when you put it into practice, the
potential that’s there transforms itself

00:16:53.819 --> 00:16:55.480
into what you’re aiming at.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.430
And that’s your territory.

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:01.699
And you need to think about it that way because
your territory can shift very rapidly on you,

00:17:01.699 --> 00:17:05.350
and if you think about it geographically,
it’s kind of confusing.

00:17:05.350 --> 00:17:08.250
So let’s say you’re on the subway that
you always take.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:11.882
And then someone sits down beside you and
they’re muttering away madly and they have

00:17:11.882 --> 00:17:14.020
a pretty malevolent look on their face.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:17.189
And you get a glimpse inside their coat and
there’s a knife.

00:17:17.189 --> 00:17:20.410
It’s like, you’re not in your territory
anymore!

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:26.870
And the fact that all of the objective entities
are still there arrayed around you is just

00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:32.960
a… it’s nothing more than a… it’s
a delusion at that point to think that those

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.730
things characterise where you are.

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:39.559
Okay so, if you’re willing to consider territory
as that…

00:17:39.559 --> 00:17:42.620
it’s a space-time continuum, okay?

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:43.960
And the reason I’m stressing that…

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:49.020
I mean, first of all, whatever reality is,
is a space-time continuum, right?

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:52.090
It’s got three dimensions of space and one
dimension of time.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:58.280
And everyone knows that, as we sit here, more
or less stable in three dimensions, time flows

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:00.289
past us, in some sense.

00:18:00.289 --> 00:18:05.409
And so the three-dimensional reality is transforming
all by itself, just because of time.

00:18:05.409 --> 00:18:11.130
Okay, and so when we’re mapping out our
territories, we map out space-time territories.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:16.410
And so, that means that not only do you know
where you are, you know when you are.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:20.110
And if you’re in your territory you know
how to act through that place and that time

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:24.820
so that you transform the potential that’s
there into whatever it is that you’re aiming

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:25.820
at.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:28.110
Okay, so that’s your territory.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:36.440
Now, that territory is isomorphic evolutionarily
with lots of things, which you could also

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:37.870
confuse geographically.

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:44.570
So you might think of a human territory as
something like… it’s probably, the fundamental

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:48.270
human territory is probably a fire.

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:53.950
And so the fire is a place of light in the
darkness and then you could think of rings,

00:18:53.950 --> 00:19:00.350
concentric rings of relative safety surrounding
the fire.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:04.140
So right beside the fire, where it’s warm
and light and there’s lots of other people,

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.840
man that’s explored territory, you know?

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:10.600
But then if you start wandering away from
the fire into the bush, especially if you’re

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:16.840
in a very biologically active area, then the
farther away you get from the fire the more

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:18.390
you’re going outside your territory.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.910
And you can tell that’s happening because
your whole nervous system starts to function

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:22.910
differently.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:29.000
What happens is that the nervous system parts
that you were using while you were in known

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:34.080
territory, they just shut down, and then other
nervous system elements that you used in unknown

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:35.860
territory start to turn on.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.770
And generally you’re not very happy about
that.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:46.610
Because when you’re aware you’ve mastered
the territory, you can presume that neutral

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:51.480
or moderately good things are going to happen,
with the possibility of something great now

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:53.070
and then.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:58.640
But as you move away from that, it flips in
some sense to “I don’t know what’s going

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:02.610
on here and I might fall” to “something’s
going to bite me” to “I’m going to die

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:08.270
a painful death”, you know, and you don’t
use the same circuits to process the first

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:10.670
situation to process the first situation as
you do to process the second.

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:14.130
You don’t even like having the second one
on.

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:18.840
So you know what it’s like… maybe you’ve
been feeling a bit down, because that always

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:22.789
helps, and you wake up at three in the morning
and you’re worrying about six things and

00:20:22.789 --> 00:20:26.880
you hear some random noise somewhere there
shouldn’t be a random noise.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:33.110
Well, how you feel right then, that’s what
it is like to have the system that deals with

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:34.500
the unexpected ‘on’.

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.539
You’re like a prey animal.

00:20:36.539 --> 00:20:41.020
Really, really, exactly like one, because
that’s what you actually are in many ways.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:46.240
So you know, your whole biology transforms
so that you’re prepared for God only knows

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:47.250
what.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:51.789
Well, what seemed to have happened in the
course of evolution was that, when we were

00:20:51.789 --> 00:21:01.380
smaller creatures, and far after that, before
let’s say we figured out how to use throwing

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:09.289
sticks and slings, and then later maybe bows
and arrows, or maybe even sticks, like, we

00:21:09.289 --> 00:21:10.740
were getting eaten a lot.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:14.830
I think I told you guys last week that they
just discovered this damn cat in Africa that

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.470
looks like it preyed on our fore-bearers.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:21.490
And we have pretty hard skulls so the normal
cat can’t really gnaw through them, but

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:24.779
this cat had a special set of teeth, and if
it grabbed the front of your head it would

00:21:24.779 --> 00:21:27.830
drive one of its teeth right through the back
of your skull.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:32.610
So you know, the hominids were preyed on enough
so that they had their own predators.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:37.230
Well, so you know, that’s just not very
pleasant.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:39.380
And then, that’s by the time we were hominids.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:43.110
You can go back farther than that to when
we were little tree rats, roughly speaking,

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:44.770
and then all sorts of things were eating us.

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:49.760
There were a lot of reptiles back then, sixty
million years ago, and they weren’t pleasant.

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:54.210
There were still dinosaurs around sixty million
years ago.

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:59.080
You know, that’s a lot of evolutionary time
for us to have developed biological systems

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:03.440
that distinguish between places we’re safe
and places we’re not safe.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:12.480
Well, you roughly have one set of neuropsychological,
neuropsychophysiological functions for familiar

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.210
territory and another set for unfamiliar territory.

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:21.160
Now what I would say… this is where this
becomes, I think, radically different to terror

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:27.560
management theory, what these hierarchy is
doing when it’s functioning is shutting

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:32.570
off… is keeping a system that operates in
unexplored territory shut off.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:33.570
And you like that.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:36.419
And that’s when you’re calm.

00:22:36.419 --> 00:22:41.120
Like you are in here, you’re relatively
at home here, because you assume that you

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:45.909
know how to act here, as long as you sit and
ask questions more or less appropriately and

00:22:45.909 --> 00:22:49.190
you don’t bother people and you don’t
talk on your phone, and various other things,

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:55.520
the probability that you’re going to confronted
by an angry primate or some other predator

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.679
is very, very low.

00:22:57.679 --> 00:23:03.210
And so, you’d say, well you’re normal.

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:05.059
You feel like you do normally here.

00:23:05.059 --> 00:23:08.970
But I would say the only reason you feel that
way normally to begin with is because you

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:12.220
don’t go places where you feel not normal.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:13.520
You stay away from them.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:17.309
And everyone knows what those places are like.

00:23:17.309 --> 00:23:20.669
I saw a real cool example of this once.

00:23:20.669 --> 00:23:26.620
I was down by OCAD at about midnight on a
Saturday night.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:32.159
You know, it was in the summer so there were
all these kids, really, wandering around in

00:23:32.159 --> 00:23:34.700
their party clothes and going to the clubs.

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:40.610
And I was standing there watching and I looked
through a park at this street that runs by

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:44.559
near OCAD and there were these houses.

00:23:44.559 --> 00:23:49.419
There was a row of houses and there were three
houses here and they were all really nicely

00:23:49.419 --> 00:23:52.820
taken care of, then there was a house here,
and then there were some more houses over

00:23:52.820 --> 00:23:54.299
here that were well taken care of.

00:23:54.299 --> 00:23:57.289
And that house in the middle was condemned.

00:23:57.289 --> 00:24:01.100
So the roof was rotting and they’d boarded
up all the windows, and the fence was falling

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:05.320
off and there was garbage in the yard, it
was a derelict place, you know.

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.559
So that’s kind of a spooky place.

00:24:07.559 --> 00:24:13.470
Well, while I was standing there this guy
walked down the street, and he was very intoxicated,

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.140
and he wasn’t very happy and he was sort
of muttering to himself.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:20.470
And he passed the three houses that were in
pretty good shape, and then as soon as he

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:25.120
got in front of the house that was derelict,
he just started to rant, and he was pounding

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:29.909
up and down and muttering paranoid things,
and you know, sounding really dangerous.

00:24:29.909 --> 00:24:34.809
But he kept more or less moving forward, and
then just after he got back past that borderline

00:24:34.809 --> 00:24:39.610
and back into the more or less normal neighbourhood,
he switched back to just mumbling quietly

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:40.610
and kept moving forward.

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:43.820
And you know he was just so damn intoxicated,
there isn’t a possibility that he noticed

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:48.260
what had happened, but he was being cued by
the surroundings, you know, and it was a different

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:54.419
‘him’ when he was in unexplored territory,
something that had degenerated back into chaos.

00:24:54.419 --> 00:24:58.029
So the anger and aggression came out of him
because that’s often necessary if you’re

00:24:58.029 --> 00:25:00.460
going to be where there’s chaos.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:04.720
You know, and chaos can burst forth, and does,
all the time in people’s lives, you know.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.970
If you’re sitting around the dinner table
with your family, and everything’s going

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:12.820
pretty well but someone touches on one of
those topics that you should just shut the

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:16.710
hell up about, it can take almost no time
at all before the whole thing unravels, and

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:19.169
then God only knows where you are.

00:25:19.169 --> 00:25:20.880
And it’s not pleasant.

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:28.320
So instead of our belief systems protecting
us from death anxiety, our hierarchical pragmatic

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:33.860
systems for interacting with the world stop
the parts of our brain that deal with the

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:35.919
unexpected from being on.

00:25:35.919 --> 00:25:42.290
And they don’t do that because we believe,
they do that because when we enact those structures,

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:44.880
all hell doesn’t break loose.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:46.770
So there’s nothing illusory about it.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:50.580
Now, you know, you might say, well, in the
final analysis, does it really protect you

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:51.899
from chaos?

00:25:51.899 --> 00:25:56.850
And you know, that’s an okay question, but
most of the time that’s irrelevant.

00:25:56.850 --> 00:26:02.060
It isn’t always irrelevant, but most of
the time it just doesn’t matter.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:08.080
And the other thing is that much of the time
it doesn’t even matter if you believe this.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:14.549
By which I would mean, you have an articulated
representation of it, it’s also a self-representation,

00:26:14.549 --> 00:26:18.340
and you carry that around, and maybe you have
a crisis of faith and you don’t believe

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.470
in that, you still act all this stuff out.

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:23.520
And most of the time you’re still extremely
well-protected by it.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:29.280
So it’s more robust than merely the articulated
or imagined version.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:30.710
It’s a lot deeper than that.

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:33.130
It’s only a tiny part of its structure.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:38.419
And so, we should never really talk about
‘belief systems’ exactly, especially not

00:26:38.419 --> 00:26:43.610
if they’re shared with very many other people,
because they’re not structures of belief,

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:45.820
they’re much deeper structures than that.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:50.980
And the belief part is only a pale reflection
of the underlying reality.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:52.700
Okay, so that…

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:56.520
I presume that makes sense, and you can make
sense of that in your own life.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:27:00.169
You know, there’s other things that can
happen too that I should mention, is lots

00:27:00.169 --> 00:27:05.450
of times people have chaos… you know, you
think your body is explored territory, it’s

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:06.970
sort of the ultimate explored territory.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:11.480
But I would say, don’t get too convinced
about that.

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:15.039
That can also switch in a moment, and it happens
to people all the time.

00:27:15.039 --> 00:27:20.720
So if you get a tooth pulled, it takes your
tongue like three months to map the inside

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:22.080
of your mouth again.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:26.769
So that’s re-familiarising your brain with
the new territory, right?

00:27:26.769 --> 00:27:28.370
And that’s very, very complicated.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:31.130
So your body representation is extremely complicated.

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:34.330
And it’s really easy to screw up.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:37.100
So if you have someone go like that [crosses
forearms and hands and fingers] and then you

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:41.049
point to one of their fingers in the middle
and get them to move it (don’t touch it),

00:27:41.049 --> 00:27:46.059
they often can’t, because just that’s
enough to boggle up their visual representation

00:27:46.059 --> 00:27:47.130
of their body.

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:51.580
And so you can play all sorts of tricks on
people that way.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:57.460
So you know, your map of your body and your
body are sort of reasonably well associated,

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:03.659
but they can be dissociated quite rapidly,
and you know that happens when, maybe you

00:28:03.659 --> 00:28:06.570
get a little hypochondriacal and start worrying
about your health.

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:09.530
Something’s bugging you and you don’t
exactly know what it is.

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:13.700
It’s like, well, all of a sudden you get
alienated from your body.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:15.450
And that can get really serious.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:21.860
And I’ve seen people after a car accident,
this was the most striking of this, this client

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:25.960
I had had her shoulder up like this [stiffens
right shoulder].

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:30.120
And I noticed she never really looked in the
direction of that arm and when she moved it

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:31.539
she’d only move it about this far.

00:28:31.539 --> 00:28:35.740
And after seeing her for a while I thought
“oh, she’s afraid of that arm”.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:37.850
Now it had been hurt.

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:39.779
Well why would you get afraid of your arm?

00:28:39.779 --> 00:28:43.320
Well, it doesn’t act like it used to, so
that’s annoying.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:47.460
And then when you move it wrong it hurts,
and then you also don’t know that if you

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:50.840
move it wrong and it hurts, whether you’re
making it worse.

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:55.059
So then you just kind of ignore it, and then
it’s sort of your arm and sort of not your

00:28:55.059 --> 00:28:56.059
arm.

00:28:56.059 --> 00:29:00.990
And I had her at one point, we just practiced
just doing this [loosens shoulder].

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.620
I’d say, I’d do the action and say “can
you do this?”

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:04.620
[Raises left arm slightly].

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:05.620
She’d do it.

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:06.620
And then I’d go “well can you do this?”

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:07.620
[Raises arm slightly higher].

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:08.620
And she’d do it.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:09.620
And then I’d say “can you do this?”

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:10.620
[Raises arm even higher].

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.860
We just kept doing this, it only took about
half an hour until she was up here [arm fully

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:17.340
raised], and she said “I haven’t put my
arm up there for two years!”

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.159
And then I did the same thing with her shoulder.

00:29:19.159 --> 00:29:22.740
I said, “Can you lift your shoulder?”

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:23.740
“No.”

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:25.760
“Can you lift it one-sixteenth of an inch?”

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.250
Well, she could do that.

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:31.450
Well, you know, sixteen times one-sixteenth
of an inch is an inch, and that’s starting

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:32.450
to get there.

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:35.340
And you know, she did this a bunch and then
her arm went down.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:39.049
So she’d been holding the damn arm up for
two years.

00:29:39.049 --> 00:29:40.769
Just try that.

00:29:40.769 --> 00:29:44.350
You know, I mean you do this [raises arm]
for five minutes and see what happens, just

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:48.299
hold your shoulder up there for two years
and see what that does to the entire right

00:29:48.299 --> 00:29:49.299
side of your body.

00:29:49.299 --> 00:29:51.100
Like it’s really, really stressful.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:56.919
And then I did something strange, then, which
was a whim, but I think it was because I was

00:29:56.919 --> 00:29:59.169
embodying her because I was paying so close
attention.

00:29:59.169 --> 00:30:02.740
I asked her to come lean over my desk because
there was something about the way she was

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.220
holding herself that was really bugging me.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:09.700
And so I asked her just to lean over my desk
and I pounded on her back down her spinal

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:14.340
cord and she drooped her shoulders again and
then she cried for about half an hour.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:19.409
And again, it was because, she’d got hit
by a car, that’s just how she was, she just

00:30:19.409 --> 00:30:23.680
walked around like that, permanently, more
or less.

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:27.429
And we worked it out of her reasonably well.

00:30:27.429 --> 00:30:32.250
But even your own damn body can become unexplored
territory.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:37.820
And that’s another way of understanding
that yin-yang symbol, right?

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:43.350
You’ve got the black serpent, paisley, and
it’s got a little white dot in it.

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:48.260
Which means that sometimes when things are
really broken all to hell a solution can come

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:52.110
blasting forth and chaos turns into order.

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:58.590
But more likely, you know, everything’s
going pretty nicely and up something comes

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:02.389
and the chaos spills into the order.

00:31:02.389 --> 00:31:04.460
And that’s the world you live in.

00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:06.720
And that’s not a metaphor.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:10.840
And it’s really interesting to know that,
because then the question becomes “okay,

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:13.980
well, what do you do in order and what do
you do in chaos?’

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:16.010
Those are useful things to know.

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:19.720
We kind of know what to do in order because
that sort of defines order.

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:23.769
But we don’t really know what to do in chaos,
you know.

00:31:23.769 --> 00:31:28.730
In fact the field of mental health is in some
sense an attempt to figure that out.

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:32.340
But I can tell you some things to do when
you’re in chaos.

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:35.430
So let’s say you’re dealing with someone
who’s very, very ill.

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:38.010
Or maybe it’s you that’s very, very ill.

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.530
And so God only knows what’s going to happen
in the future.

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:43.050
Or even next week.

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:47.779
Well, the first thing you do is stop thinking
about next week and the future.

00:31:47.779 --> 00:31:50.549
Because it’s not calculable, right?

00:31:50.549 --> 00:31:57.419
The pathways have become so multiple and so
uncontrollable that if you think about them

00:31:57.419 --> 00:31:59.740
it’s just going to hurt you.

00:31:59.740 --> 00:32:04.400
And so, you know, you can’t just not think
about it, because that doesn’t work.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:08.019
But what you can do is narrow your time-frame
of analysis.

00:32:08.019 --> 00:32:11.200
You know, so instead of worrying about the
week, you think okay, well let’s make this

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.450
day as good as it can be.

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.460
And then if you’re really stressed, it’s
like, no we’re going to really work on this

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:17.990
hour.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:25.420
And so what happens is you hyper-attend to
the local space-time area and that’s what

00:32:25.420 --> 00:32:26.420
you’ve got.

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:27.420
You can work with that.

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:28.420
And it really works.

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:31.120
Like if you’re dealing with people who are
dying, for example, that’s what you do.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:37.250
You zero in to the right level of resolution,
fundamentally.

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:42.200
And you can tell when it’s the right level
of resolution because you don’t feel like

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:43.210
you don’t know what you’re doing anymore.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:46.490
“What am I doing here, what am I supposed
to do here, what does all this mean?”

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:47.679
You don’t feel that anymore.

00:32:47.679 --> 00:32:50.780
Because those are the wrong questions at that
point in time.

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:54.740
And you know, maybe you try to make the person
comfortable, and you pay attention, so you

00:32:54.740 --> 00:33:00.400
can take the right micro-movements and the
right micro-attitudes in those critical moments,

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.100
and that’s what you do.

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:05.930
And so that’s one way of dealing with the
underworld and you need to know that, because

00:33:05.930 --> 00:33:08.299
it’s going to happen.

00:33:08.299 --> 00:33:13.770
So if you’re really stressed, you think
about smaller amounts of space and time.

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:19.169
And you try to get them right, it’s still
important to get it right, but the entropy

00:33:19.169 --> 00:33:24.559
is so high at that point, the landscape is
so complicated, that there’s no that you

00:33:24.559 --> 00:33:28.760
can map more than a few moments at a time.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:31.250
But it turns out often that that’s okay.

00:33:31.250 --> 00:33:32.929
That’s good enough.

00:33:32.929 --> 00:33:37.250
You know, obviously it’s not optimal, but
one thing you can be sure of is you can take

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:41.799
a terrible situation and you can make it a
hell of a lot worse.

00:33:41.799 --> 00:33:46.010
So you know, if it’s a terrible situation
and you manage it as well as it can be managed,

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:48.019
then maybe it’s only terrible.

00:33:48.019 --> 00:33:51.899
But if you take a terrible situation and you
manage it really badly, then it can be something

00:33:51.899 --> 00:33:53.980
that you never, ever recover from.

00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:59.519
So, I’m not trying to be hyper-optimistic
about this, I know people go through rough

00:33:59.519 --> 00:34:00.519
times.

00:34:00.519 --> 00:34:03.070
But there’s a difference between tragedy
and hell.

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:05.909
And tragedy is when something happens and
you’re awake.

00:34:05.909 --> 00:34:10.810
And hell is when something terrible happens
and you’re not awake.

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:13.450
And that’s a place you really don’t want
to go.

00:34:13.450 --> 00:34:17.620
So then the death of your father could turn
into the obliteration of your entire family.

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:22.680
Well, you know, the first is bad enough, but
the second might well be unbearable.

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:27.670
You know, because people often fight in circumstances
like that, and the family gets very, very

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:28.670
stressed.

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:32.820
It’s very common, for example, if a couple
has a sick child or a child that dies that

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:34.000
they get divorced.

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:35.970
It’s not surprising, you know.

00:34:35.970 --> 00:34:40.100
It’s not like they have a psychological
problem, they have a real problem.

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:44.410
And they might be amplifying it with their
psychological problems, but all problems are

00:34:44.410 --> 00:34:45.730
not psychological.

00:34:45.730 --> 00:34:50.740
And that’s also useful to know, because
if you end up somewhere that you don’t expect,

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:54.610
you’re going to be thinking (especially
if you’re conscientious): “If I wasn’t

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:58.520
such a useless fool, I would have never ended
up here”.

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:02.521
And there’s a much more pessimistic thought
than that that’s much more useful, which

00:35:02.521 --> 00:35:07.380
is: whether you were a fool or not you were
going to end up somewhere like this now and

00:35:07.380 --> 00:35:08.380
then.

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:13.420
And you might think, God, that’s a horrible
thing to think, because that means that our

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:16.210
destinies are uncontrollable, at least in
part.

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:18.110
It’s like, yeah they are.

00:35:18.110 --> 00:35:22.710
So partly what that means is prepare yourself
for that, and don’t pound yourself into

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:25.070
the ground when you get there.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:29.470
Because it happens to people, and it isn’t
necessarily the case that the reason it happened

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:31.020
to you is because you’re useless.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:35.050
Now, that might have furthered the process
and all that, but you just don’t need those

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:37.440
thoughts at that time.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:39.010
All they do is undermine you.

00:35:39.010 --> 00:35:46.450
And when you know “yeah, you know, everybody’s
dog dies” (which is true), it’s like,

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:53.160
well, it takes some of the guilt out of it,
and the guilt is something that at that point

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:54.910
you don’t really need.

00:35:54.910 --> 00:36:01.000
Okay, so, you’ve got your explored territory,
and we’ve kind of walked through what that

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:04.330
is, and then you have your unexplored territory.

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:07.310
And then you have the things that sort of
exist on the fringe.

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:11.780
I would say in some sense the things that
exist on the fringe closer to the unknown,

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:15.770
those are usually symbolised by the Terrible
Mother.

00:36:15.770 --> 00:36:18.440
And then the order is symbolised by the Terrible
Father.

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:22.520
And then the chaos that’s beyond order…

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:26.560
the unknown that’s beyond order and chaos,
that’s the Dragon of Chaos.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:29.400
Now it’s a reptile, right, a reptilian carnivore.

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:33.290
It’s a reptilian carnivore that has a variety
of interesting properties.

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:39.560
So let me show you some of the pictures that
I’ve got of the dragon of chaos.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:42.930
This is a good one.

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:47.250
So that’s a figure called an ‘Ouroboros’,
and you can say in some sense that that’s

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:50.110
one of the first graphical representations
of the infinite.

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:56.600
You know, we have a symbol for infinity, right,
which is another thing that’s pretty weird

00:36:56.600 --> 00:37:00.770
to symbolise, because you’re encapsulating
something that can’t be encapsulated inside

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:02.070
a capsule, right.

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:04.080
It’s very sneaky to be able to do that.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:07.830
And you actually need it to do advanced mathematics
of all sorts of kinds.

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:13.340
So it’s real, at least insofar as you can
use it as a tool.

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:17.880
So let me tell you a few things about this
symbol, this representation.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:24.360
It’s kind of like the Judeo-Christian idea
of God being the Alpha and Omega.

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:29.230
Which is this thing that exists from the beginning
of time all the way to the end of time.

00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:32.930
So it’s something that exists outside of
time.

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:39.500
So you could think about this, at one level,
as the eternal presence of The Predator.

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:44.080
Predators come and go; The Predator never
goes away.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:48.540
So it’s got this God-like… you could say
the spirit of The Predator never disappears.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:52.430
And as soon as you think that way then you
have some idea about what archaic people were

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:55.330
doing when they were populating the world
with spirits.

00:37:55.330 --> 00:38:01.070
The difference between a spirit and an abstract
idea is like, there’s no difference.

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:03.840
The spirit of The Predator inhabits the jungle.

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.200
It’s like, yeah, that’s for sure.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:09.890
And you should be very careful of disturbing
such spirits when you go in there.

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:13.270
Well so then you might ask yourself, well,
what is a predator?

00:38:13.270 --> 00:38:20.460
Well, that’s a complicated thing, because
we would say, because we’re scientifically-minded,

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:26.030
that you can take predators and you can classify
according to their species and their sub-species

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:30.440
and their phyla and all that sort of thing,
right.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:34.060
We think about classifying them as objects,
and then maybe in terms of their evolutionary

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:35.060
origins.

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:37.830
But that’s not very helpful.

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:43.090
You know, if something jumps out and wants
to eat you, I mean, knowing what it is might

00:38:43.090 --> 00:38:46.930
help you plot your escape but all those additional
complexities, they’re just not the relevant

00:38:46.930 --> 00:38:48.200
issue at that point.

00:38:48.200 --> 00:38:53.780
The relevant issue is, how do you keep the
predators away from your explored territory?

00:38:53.780 --> 00:39:03.000
Well, now that’s a complicated question,
because you might say: what do you mean by

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:04.000
that?

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:06.200
Do you mean right now, while you’re walking
through the bush?

00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:11.650
Do you mean right now for you and your family,
while all of you are walking through the bush?

00:39:11.650 --> 00:39:17.130
There’s this old joke about these two guys
that go out in the forest, and one guy says

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:23.740
to the other: “There’s grizzly bears here,
so you have to be careful.”

00:39:23.740 --> 00:39:28.820
And then the other guy says, “Well, how
fast can you run?”

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:34.180
And the first guy says “Well, don’t you
mean how fast does the grizzly bear run?”

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:38.431
And the other guy says “No that isn’t
what I mean I just want to know if I can run

00:39:38.431 --> 00:39:39.650
faster than you.”

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:40.650
[Laughter].

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:41.690
Right, right.

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:48.650
So the reason I’m telling you that stupid
joke is because what you want as an answer

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:53.100
to a question depends on exactly how you put
the question.

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:58.490
And so, the answer there was “well I don’t
care if the grizzly bear eats someone as long

00:39:58.490 --> 00:39:59.490
as it’s not me.”

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:01.540
Okay, fine, so that’s one answer to the
question.

00:40:01.540 --> 00:40:07.010
But then you might think, well let’s keep
the grizzly bears away from me now, my family

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:16.610
now, my tribe now, me tomorrow, my family
tomorrow, my tribe tomorrow, then across the

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:20.750
week, then across the month, then across the
years, then maybe forever.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:24.500
Okay so how is it that you have to act so
that you keep the predators away?

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:28.570
Well, that starts to become an extraordinarily
complicated problem.

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:32.890
Because the simple solution might be, well
when there’s a predator around, climb a

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:36.620
tree or run into a hole, which is what lots
of animals do.

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:38.480
But it’s a pretty partial solution.

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:43.360
They don’t get eaten that day, but what
about tomorrow, when the predator’s a little

00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:44.360
hungrier.

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:47.650
It’s kind of a bone-headed solution, all
things considered.

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:53.250
So then you might say, well, if we thought
of the spirit of the predator, which is more

00:40:53.250 --> 00:41:00.240
like the fact that there is a class of predators
and they’re never going to go away, then

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:05.130
the question that we have to answer is, how
do we act so that that class that’s eternal

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:07.640
doesn’t bother us any more than necessary?

00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:09.930
Well that’s really complicated.

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:12.520
Really, really complicated.

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:15.660
Because it might mean, in some sense, well,
how should you act?

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:22.470
Because conquering the problem of having your
territory invaded, there’s not just reptilian

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:27.690
and mammalian predators, right, like bird-things
with teeth, there’s other people.

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:31.890
And they’re a lot worse than anything else
that’s going to come at you, so you also

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:36.350
have that problem, how do you keep yourself
away from the predatory people and the foreign

00:41:36.350 --> 00:41:40.390
tribes that are going to invade your land
and take it.

00:41:40.390 --> 00:41:46.660
So there’s no difference between that question
and how do you establish the security of a

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:48.470
nation, roughly speaking.

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:50.850
Or maybe even how do you establish peace?

00:41:50.850 --> 00:41:51.850
You know what I mean?

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:54.910
It’s a question that doesn’t have obvious
borders.

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:59.270
Okay, so one answer might be, stay away from
the damn predators.

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:02.930
So you build a big wall, or someone else does,
and you hide inside the walls.

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:07.420
Well, that’s not a bad solution, and of
course, that’s what people started to do

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:08.930
when they originally built cities, right?

00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:15.650
They built these big walls, like Trump is
going to build between Canada and the US,

00:42:15.650 --> 00:42:17.330
and it’ll keep the predators out.

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:22.880
But of course, what do we know from the story
of Genesis, and from the Daoist symbol?

00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:26.320
No matter how big the wall is, you can’t
keep out the damn snakes.

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:31.820
So you build a wall and all the snakes pop
up inside, so it’s not a very good solution.

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:34.090
And you know, that’s pretty obvious, right?

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:40.190
I mean, my suspicions are that mass shooters
who are native to the United States kill a

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:44.820
lot more people in the US every week than
illegal Mexican immigrants.

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:55.550
Well, so it’s like, what problem exactly
are you solving?

00:42:55.550 --> 00:43:05.330
Okay, so the first question you have to solve
when you’re… see there’s just no solution

00:43:05.330 --> 00:43:16.580
to this problem [open doors in the classroom?][Laughter].

00:43:16.580 --> 00:43:25.770
So, why did you laugh at that, do you think?

00:43:25.770 --> 00:43:28.130
[Student: Because it opened again and it was
unexpected?]

00:43:28.130 --> 00:43:29.920
Yeah, but why didn’t that terrify you?

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:33.680
Well, you know, it’s related to the hierarchy
issue, right?

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:35.660
Because the door isn’t behaving properly.

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:37.340
So what’s funny about that?

00:43:37.340 --> 00:43:45.340
Well, it’s funny to watch someone’s little
routine not work when the consequences are

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:46.340
trivial.

00:43:46.340 --> 00:43:48.310
It’s like, oh well he knows how to close
the door.

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:49.310
Hahaha it’s not working.

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:52.910
So now he’s got the problem of this door
and that’s a stupid problem, so everyone

00:43:52.910 --> 00:43:55.820
can laugh about it, you know?

00:43:55.820 --> 00:44:02.550
So, little anomalies, that you can bind quickly,
are common.

00:44:02.550 --> 00:44:06.740
And I think it’s an indication, to tell
you the truth, I think it’s an indication

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:09.530
of people’s transcendent ability.

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.830
Because what happens, comedians do this with
you too, you know.

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:16.151
So you’ll be sitting in the audience, and
the comedian will say this thing and then

00:44:16.151 --> 00:44:19.370
this thing, and they’re connected in some
way, but it’s not obvious.

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:21.090
And then you go, click!

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:24.640
And it’s like everybody celebrates their
brilliance together, like “Oh I get it!

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:25.640
Hahaha!

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:26.840
Aren’t I smart?”

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:32.030
And it transforms you from the puzzled thing
into the solving thing.

00:44:32.030 --> 00:44:33.310
And everyone really likes that.

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:34.310
It’s like, “Yes!

00:44:34.310 --> 00:44:35.310
We solved it.

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:37.730
We can go and combat the unknown together.”

00:44:37.730 --> 00:44:43.530
Okay, so you have this chaos thing here [points
to figure].

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:46.560
As I said, that’s called an Ouroboros.

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:52.530
The reason it’s got its tail in its mouth
is… it’s a… the idea that that’s trying

00:44:52.530 --> 00:44:55.510
to represent is that this thing is complete
unto itself.

00:44:55.510 --> 00:44:58.520
So it’s a symbol of totality.

00:44:58.520 --> 00:45:00.600
It’s everything.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:01.640
How?

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:05.910
Well, you walk into the room where your interview
is.

00:45:05.910 --> 00:45:07.320
You might say, what’s in there?

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:10.720
Well a better question is, what’s not in
there?

00:45:10.720 --> 00:45:15.590
That’s a better question, because your whole
damn future’s in there.

00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:17.520
And you don’t even know how much that branches.

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:18.950
Your future’s in there.

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:22.860
The future of your children is in there.

00:45:22.860 --> 00:45:23.860
The future of your grandchildren is in there.

00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:27.810
There’s these waves that go out from that
room that in some sense, especially if you

00:45:27.810 --> 00:45:31.950
stretch them forward in time, have almost
no boundary.

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:35.440
You know, I mean, I don’t know, each of
us is descended from one person.

00:45:35.440 --> 00:45:39.340
I don’t remember how many millions of years
ago, but it’s not that many.

00:45:39.340 --> 00:45:40.590
Two?

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.630
Maybe it’s even earlier than that.

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:43.630
650,000 years?

00:45:43.630 --> 00:45:44.630
Something like that.

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:48.910
So for all you know, one of you could be the
person through whom the entire human race

00:45:48.910 --> 00:45:53.420
lived, and maybe it’s because you made the
right decision in that interview.

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:58.290
My point is that potential itself has no boundary,
no obvious boundary.

00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:00.850
And you don’t know what you’re messing
with when you’re messing with it.

00:46:00.850 --> 00:46:02.600
It’s very, very complicated.

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:05.020
So how should you do it?

00:46:05.020 --> 00:46:09.120
Well, this figure says, well you better watch
out, because some of it is predatory, and

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:13.210
it crawls along the ground, and it’s got
teeth, and it’s no damn joke.

00:46:13.210 --> 00:46:18.790
And then you can think it’s sort of snake-like,
because snakes sneak around through the grass.

00:46:18.790 --> 00:46:22.790
And then it’s sort of crocodile-like, because
snakes don’t have teeth but crocodiles sure

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:25.050
the hell do, and so do other predatory lizards.

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:31.920
And you know, as we’ve talked about before,
the probability that our ancestral plains-dwelling

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:37.030
African ancestors were regularly lunched upon
by crocodiles is pretty damn high.

00:46:37.030 --> 00:46:38.810
Because how else would they get water?

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:43.010
And you’ve got to be pretty mean before
you can take on a Nile crocodile.

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:47.670
I mean, I’m sure we did that fairly early
in our evolutionary history, but before that

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:50.230
a lot of us were snacks for lizards.

00:46:50.230 --> 00:46:51.770
Which seems…

00:46:51.770 --> 00:46:56.850
just not good, you know?

00:46:56.850 --> 00:47:00.800
So this thing also lives in a hole, dragons
usually live in holes or underground, like

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:02.500
Harry Potter’s basilisk.

00:47:02.500 --> 00:47:03.770
And usually they’re immortal.

00:47:03.770 --> 00:47:06.130
They live in a cave, they’re immortal, right?

00:47:06.130 --> 00:47:09.090
They live forever, and you don’t want to
bother them, because then they pop out of

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:12.470
their hole, like they do in The Hobbit, and
they come and burn down your village.

00:47:12.470 --> 00:47:14.890
It’s like, leave the damn dragon alone.

00:47:14.890 --> 00:47:17.140
Well, yes and no.

00:47:17.140 --> 00:47:19.960
Why not leave it alone?

00:47:19.960 --> 00:47:24.250
Well, maybe it would be better to go get the
dragon before it gets you.

00:47:24.250 --> 00:47:26.180
That’s certainly possible.

00:47:26.180 --> 00:47:30.400
And then there’s this other weird thing
about dragons which is that they hoard treasure.

00:47:30.400 --> 00:47:33.490
So maybe you can go and confront the dragon
and you get the treasure.

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:35.540
And the treasure basically takes two forms.

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:43.250
So there’s the St. George story, where the
treasure is often a trapped, young female.

00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:46.340
And the other version is the dragon sits on
gold.

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:49.380
So what’s the whole idea there?

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:55.130
Well, maybe the best way to overcome the predator,
the spirit of the predator, is to confront

00:47:55.130 --> 00:47:56.130
it.

00:47:56.130 --> 00:48:00.130
Well, that’s a hell of an idea.

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:03.600
Then you might think, well how deep an idea
is that?

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:05.460
You confront the predator, you get the gold.

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:11.170
Well, this is that you confront infinite possibility
and you get the reward.

00:48:11.170 --> 00:48:14.760
You encounter infinite possibility properly.

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.620
You derive from it the rewards that it offers.

00:48:17.620 --> 00:48:21.200
What are the rewards that infinite possibility
offers?

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:23.600
Who knows?

00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:24.600
We have no idea.

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:29.350
And it’s so interesting, because we live
at a time in history where that’s literally

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:30.350
true.

00:48:30.350 --> 00:48:35.570
Like I would hesitate to predict anything
about fifty years from now.

00:48:35.570 --> 00:48:38.990
I quit worrying about the future about twenty
years ago.

00:48:38.990 --> 00:48:41.630
Because I thought, oh, I get it.

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:45.750
No matter what happens, the probability that
it’s something I predict is zero.

00:48:45.750 --> 00:48:49.550
So it’s like, whatever you’re worried
about, well, go ahead and worry about it,

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:51.810
but something completely different is going
to happen.

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:54.610
So it’s not really all that useful.

00:48:54.610 --> 00:48:59.000
You should just be ready for whatever comes,
because here it comes.

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:04.450
You know, like I think we’ll have fully-functional
autonomous robots within three or four years.

00:49:04.450 --> 00:49:07.850
And that’s a real game-changer.

00:49:07.850 --> 00:49:10.920
And God only knows what’s going to happen
after that.

00:49:10.920 --> 00:49:17.420
Transplantable organs… those things are…
you can print those on 3D printers already.

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:20.400
Well, augmented intelligence.

00:49:20.400 --> 00:49:22.640
You all have augmented intelligence already.

00:49:22.640 --> 00:49:23.640
What good are facts?

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:26.260
You just look them up, right?

00:49:26.260 --> 00:49:30.230
Everyone’s an infinite supply of facts.

00:49:30.230 --> 00:49:38.220
[Student: Can you explain, in different words,
why the dragon is eating its own tail?]

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:40.420
Because it has everything it needs.

00:49:40.420 --> 00:49:42.930
That’s another way of thinking about it.

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:45.700
So it’s self-contained.

00:49:45.700 --> 00:49:50.550
It doesn’t need anything outside of itself,
and there isn’t anything outside of itself.

00:49:50.550 --> 00:49:54.090
So it doesn’t rely on anything to exist.

00:49:54.090 --> 00:49:55.500
That’s another way of thinking about it.

00:49:55.500 --> 00:49:56.820
It’s just there.

00:49:56.820 --> 00:50:03.950
And so there’s an echo there of the idea
that… of an idea of beyond time.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:07.401
Or I don’t know so much beyond space, but
certainly beyond time.

00:50:07.401 --> 00:50:10.500
Time has no effect on it.

00:50:10.500 --> 00:50:13.830
So it’s beyond time, in that sense.

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:14.830
Yeah?

00:50:14.830 --> 00:50:20.290
[Student: There’s another reason it has
its tail in its mouth.

00:50:20.290 --> 00:50:26.490
And that’s because it represents the border
between the known world, in all its goodness

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:28.540
and badness, and the unknown world.

00:50:28.540 --> 00:50:32.990
And that border can get smaller as it eats
more of its tail.]

00:50:32.990 --> 00:50:34.010
Mhm, right, right.

00:50:34.010 --> 00:50:39.080
Okay, so, now it has wings, why does it have
wings?

00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:43.350
Well, this is another horribly complicated
thing.

00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:45.770
I’ll show you a diagram.

00:50:45.770 --> 00:50:49.460
This is the closest I’ve been able to get
to it.

00:50:49.460 --> 00:51:05.900
I often don’t talk about this diagram, but
I guess I am today.

00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:26.320
Yeah, okay, we might as well go through this
completely.

00:51:26.320 --> 00:51:35.910
Okay so here’s you.

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:42.090
So if you look right in the middle there,
here I’ll make it bigger so that you can

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:45.530
see it.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:47.110
You kind of think that you’re right here.

00:51:47.110 --> 00:51:49.640
This is where your consciousness normally
sits.

00:51:49.640 --> 00:51:53.780
Like, you know, are you yourself, that’s
sort of your conscious body envelope and all

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:55.670
that, or are you your family?

00:51:55.670 --> 00:52:01.330
Well you know, the border there is rather
pliable.

00:52:01.330 --> 00:52:05.460
Well then, you can drill down into the cell,
and this is sort of the idea of hierarchy.

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:10.550
You know, you can look at principles, so those
are your general philosophical beliefs and

00:52:10.550 --> 00:52:16.140
then your programs, and the sequence of behaviours
that you instantiate that in and then your

00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:17.140
behaviours.

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:21.610
And then you can go below that even and you
can think of yourself as…

00:52:21.610 --> 00:52:25.530
well because when you’re behaving, what’s
under that?

00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:27.390
What’s the support for that?

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:28.390
The platform for that?

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:29.700
Well it’s your body, right?

00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:31.000
Well, what’s there?

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:34.990
Well, once you get past the level of arm,
it’s sort of like musculature.

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:39.900
And then you might say, what’s under that,
what’s next?

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:44.720
Well you have your cellular structure, and
so that’s what cellular biologists study,

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:46.720
God that’s so complex.

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:49.880
And then below that you have your molecular
structures, like DNA, and then underneath

00:52:49.880 --> 00:52:50.880
that there’s the atomic structure.

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:57.370
And then underneath that there’s the sub-atomic
structure, and what are you going to say about

00:52:57.370 --> 00:52:58.370
that?

00:52:58.370 --> 00:53:04.820
There isn’t even time at that level.

00:53:04.820 --> 00:53:06.630
And then you can do the same thing if you
go up.

00:53:06.630 --> 00:53:10.600
It’s like, people say you shouldn’t just
pay attention to your family, you’re in

00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:12.330
a cultural group, they’re important too.

00:53:12.330 --> 00:53:16.250
And well, what about the biological group
that that’s nested in, and maybe you should

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:20.240
be paying more attention to the ecosystem
rather than to yourself.

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:26.280
And you know, you shade upward into this thing
that you can’t really dissociate yourself

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:28.790
from either.

00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:33.450
So that’s what you are, make no mistake
about it.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:40.110
Your consciousness is such that you only are
located, in some sense, at one level of observation,

00:53:40.110 --> 00:53:42.050
and God only knows why that is.

00:53:42.050 --> 00:53:49.360
It’s almost as if being has to come to a
pinpoint in order for things to exist.

00:53:49.360 --> 00:53:53.610
The way I’ve been thinking about it, in
some sense, is you could take all of this

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:59.760
structure and you know, narrow it down to
a single point of a pencil that’s writing.

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:05.020
And the writing is the transformation of potential
into actuality, but in order to do that there

00:54:05.020 --> 00:54:11.100
has to be a point of contact between you and
the potential, and that’s your body in part,

00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:12.640
but it’s even narrower than that.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:18.730
It’s whatever occupies this tiny little
laser beam of attention while you’re wandering

00:54:18.730 --> 00:54:19.730
through the world.

00:54:19.730 --> 00:54:22.420
And it’s like burning reality into being.

00:54:22.420 --> 00:54:26.990
So why that is, I mean, God, who knows?

00:54:26.990 --> 00:54:29.390
It’s beyond strange.

00:54:29.390 --> 00:54:35.570
Now, you know, one way of thinking about this
whole structure is you could think about it

00:54:35.570 --> 00:54:40.240
as the psychological world, because you’re
at the centre of it, or you could think about

00:54:40.240 --> 00:54:44.680
it as the spiritual world if you think about
spirit as a matter of consciousness.

00:54:44.680 --> 00:54:49.540
It’s not the material world anyways, or
if it is it’s the material world insofar

00:54:49.540 --> 00:54:53.470
as it’s you as a living conscious being.

00:54:53.470 --> 00:54:57.150
So you know, we tend to separate out consciousness
and matter, right?

00:54:57.150 --> 00:55:00.260
I mean, I’m conscious, that’s something
weird.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:05.290
We don’t think this table is so we think
of them as separate substances in a sense,

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:06.290
spirit and matter.

00:55:06.290 --> 00:55:09.850
Well that’s what the dragon is: it’s half
matter, that’s the thing that’s crawling

00:55:09.850 --> 00:55:13.260
around on the ground, and it’s half spirit,
and that’s the wings.

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:19.460
And then you might say, well why the hell
is the dragon half spirit and half matter?

00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:21.220
Well we’ve got the spirit part, I just showed
you it.

00:55:21.220 --> 00:55:32.960
So now I’m going to show you the matter
part.

00:55:32.960 --> 00:55:48.060
Okay, so we could say, well what’s that?

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:51.880
Well it’s a chair.

00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:53.260
You think, that’s pretty straightforward.

00:55:53.260 --> 00:55:56.430
It’s actually not, and I can tell you why
pretty rapidly.

00:55:56.430 --> 00:55:58.650
Why is it a chair?

00:55:58.650 --> 00:56:01.650
[Student: The legs?]

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:05.670
How about a beanbag?

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:06.740
Is that a chair?

00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:09.100
That’s right because we can sit in it!

00:56:09.100 --> 00:56:12.210
And that seems to be the fundamental issue:
is this a chair?

00:56:12.210 --> 00:56:13.360
Is this a chair?

00:56:13.360 --> 00:56:15.120
[Points to desk]
[Student: It can be.]

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:16.120
It can be, yeah, yeah.

00:56:16.120 --> 00:56:18.610
You’d say it’s not a canonical chair,
and neither is a beanbag.

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:21.810
Because a chair could be a beanbag or a stump.

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:24.380
But you know, that’s sort of a canonical
chair.

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:29.580
But part of my point is, you know, the typical
answer that people will give when you ask

00:56:29.580 --> 00:56:34.170
“why is that a chair?” is that they’ll
lay out the objective structure, that’s

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:36.600
only a tiny bit of a chair.

00:56:36.600 --> 00:56:40.360
You know, if it was that high, would it be
a chair?

00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:45.530
It has to have the right structure and the
right function in order for it to be a chair.

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:50.790
And so what that means is that thing which
is an object is already only existing in relationship

00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:52.140
to you, in some important sense.

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:56.430
But I could also say well, it’s part of
the classroom.

00:56:56.430 --> 00:56:57.970
What’s the classroom part of?

00:56:57.970 --> 00:56:59.580
Well it’s part of Sid Smith.

00:56:59.580 --> 00:57:00.970
This chair is part of Sid Smith.

00:57:00.970 --> 00:57:02.490
And what’s Sid Smith part of?

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:03.650
Well it’s part of the university.

00:57:03.650 --> 00:57:05.370
And what’s the university part of?

00:57:05.370 --> 00:57:09.730
Well God, it’s part of all sorts of things,
but we could say it’s a piece of the city,

00:57:09.730 --> 00:57:15.000
it’s a chunk of the province, it’s a bit
of the country, and then it’s nested inside

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.560
a much broader civilisation than that.

00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:19.930
So that’s sort of going up from the chair.

00:57:19.930 --> 00:57:23.950
And you could say, well, that’s not really
a chair, and I could say, well if that thing

00:57:23.950 --> 00:57:29.270
was floating at some point halfway between
the Sun and Pluto, I don’t think considering

00:57:29.270 --> 00:57:31.930
it a chair would be reasonable.

00:57:31.930 --> 00:57:34.050
Because, well for obvious reasons.

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:38.370
You know, you can decontextualize something
so completely that it isn’t what it is anymore.

00:57:38.370 --> 00:57:40.800
It’s not that hard.

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:44.180
You know, and you kind of do that to yourself
when you say, well, who cares, why is this

00:57:44.180 --> 00:57:45.180
important?

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:46.610
Who’s going to know in a million years?

00:57:46.610 --> 00:57:47.610
Poof!

00:57:47.610 --> 00:57:48.610
You’re decontextualized.

00:57:48.610 --> 00:57:51.120
It’s a stupid trick, it’s like a party
trick for intellects.

00:57:51.120 --> 00:57:53.880
It’s a stupid trick, you know?

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:55.470
Because anybody can do it?

00:57:55.470 --> 00:57:56.770
And what does it mean?

00:57:56.770 --> 00:58:00.280
It means you’re looking at it from the wrong
perspective, that’s what it means.

00:58:00.280 --> 00:58:02.770
So it’s not like it’s an ‘ultimate question’.

00:58:02.770 --> 00:58:08.330
You wouldn’t be able to think about it in
fifteen seconds if it was an ultimate question.

00:58:08.330 --> 00:58:11.460
But then you could do the same thing with
the chair that you did that you did with yourself

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:12.940
when you zoom in to it, right?

00:58:12.940 --> 00:58:17.410
So it’s made of parts, well obviously, and
then the parts are made out of sub-parts and

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:20.210
so forth, all the way down to the sub-atomic
level.

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:23.460
And the chair is all those things at the same
time.

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:30.830
Okay, so then you might say, well what do
you encounter when you encounter the unknown?

00:58:30.830 --> 00:58:35.940
And that’s what you encounter, I think.

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:37.460
So let’s take a look at this.

00:58:37.460 --> 00:58:38.990
And you can think about it.

00:58:38.990 --> 00:58:41.060
It’s a weird idea.

00:58:41.060 --> 00:58:44.300
Okay, so you’ve got your little frame of
reference, right?

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:48.440
You’re going from point A to point B. And
when you’re going from point A to point

00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:51.890
B something that you didn’t want to happen
happens.

00:58:51.890 --> 00:58:55.290
And we already know that it can be a little
thing that happens, and then you know, haha

00:58:55.290 --> 00:58:59.700
it’s funny, or it can be your whole world
falls apart, and then it’s a catastrophe.

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:04.200
But let’s say it’s something like the
interview.

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:07.710
So I would say that’s sort of a mid-level
anomaly, right?

00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:12.520
You walk in, there’s a lot in there, but
you can handle it, weirdly enough.

00:59:12.520 --> 00:59:16.050
And I suppose that’s because there are other
places that you can go where there are potential

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:20.060
futures lying, so it’s not like you’re
out of potential futures just because you

00:59:20.060 --> 00:59:22.020
walk through that.

00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:26.160
So you say, well, what’s in that room is
anomalous, you don’t understand it.

00:59:26.160 --> 00:59:28.830
It’s unformed matter.

00:59:28.830 --> 00:59:34.280
Now you might say, someone might say, does
this interview matter to you?

00:59:34.280 --> 00:59:40.020
You think, that’s a pretty weird question,
because things are made out of matter.

00:59:40.020 --> 00:59:42.740
So what do you mean when you say does this
matter to you?

00:59:42.740 --> 00:59:47.220
Well, partly what it means is that before
matter was our matter, it was a matter that

00:59:47.220 --> 00:59:50.610
was more complicated, linguistically speaking.

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:57.690
So if you go back far enough, what people
meant by matter wasn’t this [raps on desk].

00:59:57.690 --> 01:00:00.990
It was more like, I would say, it’s more
like potential.

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:02.830
Or it’s more like information.

01:00:02.830 --> 01:00:04.550
That’s another way of thinking about it.

01:00:04.550 --> 01:00:08.020
So you could certainly say that room is chock-full
of information.

01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:11.700
So let’s go with the information idea, just
for the sake of argument.

01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:15.970
We already talked about it as potential, let’s
talk about it as information, both of those

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:16.990
work.

01:00:16.990 --> 01:00:19.770
You walk in there and you start interacting
with people.

01:00:19.770 --> 01:00:26.220
And then you leave later and you know, you
tell your partner “Well I went for this

01:00:26.220 --> 01:00:27.220
interview.”

01:00:27.220 --> 01:00:28.220
And they say “How did it go?”

01:00:28.220 --> 01:00:31.060
And you say “Well, it didn’t go very well
but I learned some things.”

01:00:31.060 --> 01:00:33.150
Because you could say that, right?

01:00:33.150 --> 01:00:39.090
Well so I would say you went into that room
and you extracted some spirit out of the potential.

01:00:39.090 --> 01:00:43.380
Now that’s exactly how Piaget looked at
it by the way.

01:00:43.380 --> 01:00:47.650
Well, I shouldn’t say ‘exactly’ because
it’s probably not exactly the way he looked

01:00:47.650 --> 01:00:48.650
at it.

01:00:48.650 --> 01:00:52.760
But basically what Piaget proclaimed with
children was that children will try out a

01:00:52.760 --> 01:00:57.780
little routine, and then something will happen
that doesn’t fit in their little routine.

01:00:57.780 --> 01:01:01.290
And so they’ll investigate it using whatever
tools they have at hand.

01:01:01.290 --> 01:01:06.110
Some of it’s trial and error, and some of
it’s… you know, they try out other tool-like

01:01:06.110 --> 01:01:09.480
embodiments of their body that have worked
in the past.

01:01:09.480 --> 01:01:15.920
So for example, the first thing a nine-month
old will do crawling around on the floor if

01:01:15.920 --> 01:01:20.450
it encounters something that’s roughly mouth-sized
is put it in their mouth.

01:01:20.450 --> 01:01:21.450
Why?

01:01:21.450 --> 01:01:24.370
Well they don’t know what it is, but they
kind of know how to use their mouth.

01:01:24.370 --> 01:01:27.840
So they throw it into their mouth, and then
they tongue it, and they get some sense of

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:31.320
sort of how it tastes, which is very important,
and what its shape is and all that, because

01:01:31.320 --> 01:01:35.290
they’re very wired up in their mouth, and
so they can investigate like mad.

01:01:35.290 --> 01:01:41.800
And so what they encounter is a thing and
then when they throw it in their mouth they

01:01:41.800 --> 01:01:46.180
practice doing something with it, you know,
getting its shape, so now they know something

01:01:46.180 --> 01:01:47.180
about it.

01:01:47.180 --> 01:01:50.860
So that changes them from a spiritual perspective,
a psychological perspective.

01:01:50.860 --> 01:01:55.190
Now they’re slightly different than they
were, they’ve incorporated some information,

01:01:55.190 --> 01:02:00.550
and they’ve also transformed the thing into
a specific thing with specific features.

01:02:00.550 --> 01:02:04.800
And so what you might say is that out of potential
spirit and matter arise.

01:02:04.800 --> 01:02:08.360
And that’s a very interesting way of looking
at it.

01:02:08.360 --> 01:02:11.900
Also it fits well with the phenomenological
philosophers.

01:02:11.900 --> 01:02:22.350
Because they believe that we encountered phenomena
and phenomena were those things that shone

01:02:22.350 --> 01:02:23.350
forth.

01:02:23.350 --> 01:02:25.910
And so that’s like a reversion to the idea
of meaning.

01:02:25.910 --> 01:02:28.570
Because you’ve got to think about it this
way too…

01:02:28.570 --> 01:02:34.690
it’s like when you walk into a library,
God, what’s in there?

01:02:34.690 --> 01:02:38.070
Well, it’s inexhaustible, a library.

01:02:38.070 --> 01:02:40.040
Well, how come you just don’t get exhausted?

01:02:40.040 --> 01:02:44.810
Well, it’s because you’re not actually
interacting with the whole library.

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:49.670
You’ve got this focal point like a laser
again, and you’ve made it into a focal point

01:02:49.670 --> 01:02:52.850
because you’re inside that hierarchical
structure, right?

01:02:52.850 --> 01:02:56.790
So you say, well I’m in the library to get
this book, I’m getting this book to read

01:02:56.790 --> 01:02:58.060
this chapter.

01:02:58.060 --> 01:03:00.130
So chapter, book, library.

01:03:00.130 --> 01:03:05.420
I’m going to university, I need to become
educated and get a job so I’ll be a good

01:03:05.420 --> 01:03:06.810
citizen and a good person.

01:03:06.810 --> 01:03:08.680
And so that’s what you’re doing in the
library.

01:03:08.680 --> 01:03:10.710
You’re just doing one little fragment of
that.

01:03:10.710 --> 01:03:15.392
And the more you have that whole model developed,
right, all the way from good person all the

01:03:15.392 --> 01:03:20.340
way down to why you’re in the library, the
more precise your aim is, the more you know

01:03:20.340 --> 01:03:24.520
what you’re doing, the less encumbered by
doubt and uncertainty you are, and the higher

01:03:24.520 --> 01:03:31.220
probability you have of focusing on your task
and then writing the path that you want to

01:03:31.220 --> 01:03:32.440
be into being.

01:03:32.440 --> 01:03:37.150
And so, well, so it’s very useful to think
about things this way and it’s also very

01:03:37.150 --> 01:03:39.380
interesting to note that that’s how you
act.

01:03:39.380 --> 01:03:41.790
You actually act like the world is made this
way.

01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:46.170
And it’s even weirder because, and this
is the case I made for you when we were talking

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:48.030
about the structure of the brain.

01:03:48.030 --> 01:03:52.910
It actually looks like this is how your brain
construes the world.

01:03:52.910 --> 01:03:56.390
Because you have systems, you’ve got a system
that works when you know what you’re doing

01:03:56.390 --> 01:03:59.460
and you’ve got the other system that works
when you don’t know what you’re doing.

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:04.840
And so basically the world is divided, just
like the Daoists say, into the orderly domain

01:04:04.840 --> 01:04:06.490
and the chaotic domain.

01:04:06.490 --> 01:04:10.410
And you need to know how to operate in both,
and you need to balance them.

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:12.340
And you need to transform one into the other.

01:04:12.340 --> 01:04:16.210
And that’s basically what you’re doing
when you’re interacting with reality.

01:04:16.210 --> 01:04:22.560
And you know, one of the things that I will
claim, and this will become more important

01:04:22.560 --> 01:04:27.420
as the course progresses, is that when you’re
doing something meaningful, you’re doing

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:28.820
that properly.

01:04:28.820 --> 01:04:35.460
And the sense of meaning that you have, is
actually an indication that your psychophysiology

01:04:35.460 --> 01:04:40.330
is manifesting that you’re in the right
place at the right time doing the right thing.

01:04:40.330 --> 01:04:41.900
And it’s real.

01:04:41.900 --> 01:04:45.460
It’s not only real, it’s like it’s the
most real thing.

01:04:45.460 --> 01:04:50.730
It’s where potential is transformed into
reality.

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:53.301
And it’s hard to get any more real than
that.

01:04:53.301 --> 01:04:57.360
So that’s partly why I’m telling you that
these things aren’t exactly metaphors, you

01:04:57.360 --> 01:04:58.360
know.

01:04:58.360 --> 01:05:01.270
They have to be represented by metaphor in
some sense.

01:05:01.270 --> 01:05:06.300
But the reality that’s underlying them…
it’s not just a story.

01:05:06.300 --> 01:05:08.980
Even though it is a story, it’s not just
a story.

01:05:08.980 --> 01:05:12.780
It’s a real representation of reality.

01:05:12.780 --> 01:05:16.882
So, you know, the other thing I kind of outlined
here is that when you hit an anomaly… you

01:05:16.882 --> 01:05:23.940
know an anomaly is sort of the thing from
which everything shines forth, when it’s

01:05:23.940 --> 01:05:28.490
still really anomalous what happens is that
it manifests itself to you in sort of like

01:05:28.490 --> 01:05:31.300
a burst of contradictory meanings.

01:05:31.300 --> 01:05:33.290
And that can be paralysing.

01:05:33.290 --> 01:05:36.810
You know, you could be really, really, really
excited and really, really, really nervous.

01:05:36.810 --> 01:05:40.620
Which might happen, for example, if you were
going to a job interview and you actually

01:05:40.620 --> 01:05:41.620
wanted the job.

01:05:41.620 --> 01:05:47.020
It would be even worse if you went to a job
interview and like 30% of you wanted the job

01:05:47.020 --> 01:05:52.300
because that’s the 30% of you that wants
to develop, and another 30% would just as

01:05:52.300 --> 01:05:55.830
soon fail so that you can crawl under your
bed at home and stay there with the dust bunnies

01:05:55.830 --> 01:05:56.830
until you’re forty.

01:05:56.830 --> 01:06:01.820
And another 25% of you is resentful about
the fact that you have to get a stupid job

01:06:01.820 --> 01:06:02.880
at all.

01:06:02.880 --> 01:06:06.320
And so then you go in there and it’s really,
really complicated.

01:06:06.320 --> 01:06:11.560
Because not only is all that potential there
but you’re fractured and those fractures

01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:16.420
make the whole situation way more complicated
and unstable than it would otherwise have

01:06:16.420 --> 01:06:17.540
to be.

01:06:17.540 --> 01:06:20.380
And that happens to people all the time.

01:06:20.380 --> 01:06:23.550
I mean, people hedge their bets in job interviews
all the time.

01:06:23.550 --> 01:06:25.880
Because they think “eh, I don’t really
care.”

01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:28.580
It’s like no, you care.

01:06:28.580 --> 01:06:31.870
You just want to have a story to tell yourself
if you fail.

01:06:31.870 --> 01:06:35.770
Well, and then if you have that story, well
then probably you’re going to fail, because

01:06:35.770 --> 01:06:36.770
you don’t get to do that.

01:06:36.770 --> 01:06:40.550
You can’t go in there at 60% and hope to
win.

01:06:40.550 --> 01:06:42.610
You have to go in there at 100%.

01:06:42.610 --> 01:06:47.281
Now that’s cool too, because what it implies
is just what the clinical psychologists have

01:06:47.281 --> 01:06:52.270
been telling people for sixty years or seventy
years.

01:06:52.270 --> 01:06:55.530
You have to voluntarily face the things that
you’re afraid of.

01:06:55.530 --> 01:06:59.030
Now it’s a little more complicated than
that because otherwise you could just go play

01:06:59.030 --> 01:07:00.560
in traffic.

01:07:00.560 --> 01:07:02.910
So it’s not a call to stupid adventure.

01:07:02.910 --> 01:07:08.710
What it does is assume that you’ve got a
direction and the direction is a valid direction.

01:07:08.710 --> 01:07:15.560
And then what happens is, as you pursue that,
obstacles emerge, anomalous, threatening obstacles,

01:07:15.560 --> 01:07:20.570
and then you either confront them, or you
abandon the whole project.

01:07:20.570 --> 01:07:25.180
Well generally speaking if you chop them into
little pieces then you can confront them and

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:26.900
digest them.

01:07:26.900 --> 01:07:29.680
And then you get stronger because you’re
doing all this digesting.

01:07:29.680 --> 01:07:33.720
It’s like you’re eating the monster of
chaos.

01:07:33.720 --> 01:07:38.610
And that’s a classic metaphor for developing
wisdom to ingest.

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:40.680
Piaget even uses that, right?

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:41.680
Assimilate.

01:07:41.680 --> 01:07:43.460
It’s exactly the same idea as ingesting.

01:07:43.460 --> 01:07:47.870
What you’re eating is information instead
of matter.

01:07:47.870 --> 01:07:51.890
And the information restructures you and makes
you more informed.

01:07:51.890 --> 01:07:56.270
And that puts you more in formation.

01:07:56.270 --> 01:08:00.330
So you know you encounter the anomaly and
it’s a burst of contradictory potential,

01:08:00.330 --> 01:08:02.930
and so that’s exactly how you respond to
it.

01:08:02.930 --> 01:08:07.210
Your body’s going everywhere at once, because
God only knows what’s going to happen.

01:08:07.210 --> 01:08:09.760
And then as you interact with it, it collapses.

01:08:09.760 --> 01:08:10.760
You’re collapsing it.

01:08:10.760 --> 01:08:13.530
That’s what happens when you take control
of the situation.

01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:19.420
And then you reduce it to a single path where
everything is going properly.

01:08:19.420 --> 01:08:24.340
And you might say well you’re bending it
to your own selfish will, but that’s not

01:08:24.340 --> 01:08:25.340
right.

01:08:25.340 --> 01:08:27.601
Because if you’re in… let’s say you’re
in an interview and there’s five or six

01:08:27.601 --> 01:08:32.050
people in there, you can’t just bend the
damn thing to your will, because you have

01:08:32.050 --> 01:08:33.980
to interact with those people.

01:08:33.980 --> 01:08:37.660
And so if you don’t do that with… you
know, you could be a psychopath and manipulate,

01:08:37.660 --> 01:08:41.510
but that’ll only work for a short period
of time, you’ll get caught, and then you’ll

01:08:41.510 --> 01:08:46.200
have to move, because that’s what psychopaths
do, that’s how they keep being psychopaths.

01:08:46.200 --> 01:08:49.839
They exhaust everyone’s trust and then they
have to move.

01:08:49.839 --> 01:08:54.339
And then they go find some other people who
are trustworthy because they build up trust

01:08:54.339 --> 01:08:57.290
capital with other people and then they betray
them.

01:08:57.290 --> 01:08:58.290
But they have to keep moving.

01:08:58.290 --> 01:09:00.549
So it’s an unstable solution.

01:09:00.549 --> 01:09:04.060
So you go in there and you do it properly.

01:09:04.060 --> 01:09:05.660
Well what are you doing exactly?

01:09:05.660 --> 01:09:06.930
You know?

01:09:06.930 --> 01:09:11.620
I would say that if you’re really, really
nailing that interview, you’re saying, well

01:09:11.620 --> 01:09:16.390
here’s a future that we could all march
together into confidently and happily.

01:09:16.390 --> 01:09:18.750
That’s what you’re saying.

01:09:18.750 --> 01:09:19.910
And I’m the person to do that.

01:09:19.910 --> 01:09:22.530
Now you might be saying that on a relatively
low scale.

01:09:22.530 --> 01:09:27.080
Like, if I’m your assistant you’re never
going to have to worry about me.

01:09:27.080 --> 01:09:29.770
Because I’m always going to be there.

01:09:29.770 --> 01:09:35.470
And then maybe if you’re the CEO, you’re
going to say well, you know, this is the way

01:09:35.470 --> 01:09:38.839
that all of us are going, and here’s why,
and this is what it’s going to look like.

01:09:38.839 --> 01:09:46.040
And the better you are at that, the more that
potential will turn into… it multiplies,

01:09:46.040 --> 01:09:47.220
that’s what happens.

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:52.800
If you’re good at dealing with it the potential
that’s accessible to you multiplies.

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:56.130
Which is really cool.

01:09:56.130 --> 01:09:58.570
And also how you’d hope it would be.

01:09:58.570 --> 01:10:01.980
Because you know, you’d hope that more potential
would manifest itself to people who are more

01:10:01.980 --> 01:10:02.980
competent.

01:10:02.980 --> 01:10:07.290
Well, if things work well that would be good.

01:10:07.290 --> 01:10:13.230
So that’s the spirit and matter idea.

01:10:13.230 --> 01:10:17.790
And so that’s represented in that dragon.

01:10:17.790 --> 01:10:27.210
So let’s go back to it.

01:10:27.210 --> 01:10:36.400
Now, okay, so you remember this character
here.

01:10:36.400 --> 01:10:37.520
So what to do about that?

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:41.060
What to do about chaos?

01:10:41.060 --> 01:10:46.710
Well one thing you might want to start by
doing is admitting that it exists.

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:51.220
So let’s say that you’re not a terror
management advocate.

01:10:51.220 --> 01:10:53.900
Let’s say you’re an advocate of positive
illusions.

01:10:53.900 --> 01:10:55.541
Does everyone know what those are?

01:10:55.541 --> 01:11:01.460
Are you still taught about those in social
psychology, positive illusions?

01:11:01.460 --> 01:11:02.460
Yes?

01:11:02.460 --> 01:11:03.460
No.

01:11:03.460 --> 01:11:05.710
How many people know about them?

01:11:05.710 --> 01:11:07.830
Okay, where did you learn about them?

01:11:07.830 --> 01:11:10.230
[Student: In your class, PSY230.]

01:11:10.230 --> 01:11:11.230
In 230?

01:11:11.230 --> 01:11:16.050
No one else has taught you about this?

01:11:16.050 --> 01:11:18.380
Anybody else learn it anywhere else?
[Student: I learned it online.]

01:11:18.380 --> 01:11:19.380
You learned it online.

01:11:19.380 --> 01:11:21.640
Okay, well thank God for that.

01:11:21.640 --> 01:11:27.480
Well, what happened was, the paper where positive
illusions were first touted, and that was

01:11:27.480 --> 01:11:32.040
in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology,
it was the most cited… was it the most cited

01:11:32.040 --> 01:11:33.880
paper ever in social psychology?

01:11:33.880 --> 01:11:35.190
It’s up there.

01:11:35.190 --> 01:11:37.370
And it’s an appalling paper!

01:11:37.370 --> 01:11:43.270
Because it basically says… it says something
like this, if you scrape away all the nonsense:

01:11:43.270 --> 01:11:46.840
things are so horrible that if you just looked
at them, you’ll be so depressed you’ll

01:11:46.840 --> 01:11:47.840
want to die.

01:11:47.840 --> 01:11:48.840
So you better not.

01:11:48.840 --> 01:11:53.290
You better weave around yourself an illusion
that things are better than they are and that

01:11:53.290 --> 01:11:56.270
you’re more powerful and wonderful than
you actually are.

01:11:56.270 --> 01:12:01.410
And if you do that, but not too much, then
you’re going to be happier.

01:12:01.410 --> 01:12:04.920
Well, you know, the errors in that are just
manifold.

01:12:04.920 --> 01:12:07.640
First it’s like, well, who cares if you’re
happy, what the hell does that have to do

01:12:07.640 --> 01:12:08.640
with anything?

01:12:08.640 --> 01:12:11.190
You’d be happy stepping on ants.

01:12:11.190 --> 01:12:12.190
[Laughter].

01:12:12.190 --> 01:12:13.190
Well really!

01:12:13.190 --> 01:12:14.800
It’s like what the hell stupid idea is that?

01:12:14.800 --> 01:12:16.630
You’ve got positive emotion.

01:12:16.630 --> 01:12:17.790
Cocaine will do that.

01:12:17.790 --> 01:12:21.410
You know, bullies are pretty happy when they’re
pounding out someone like Milhouse.

01:12:21.410 --> 01:12:25.630
So you know, happiness, what the hell’s
that?

01:12:25.630 --> 01:12:26.880
It’s shallow.

01:12:26.880 --> 01:12:29.370
It’s a dopey idea.

01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:33.560
And then, well, how big should these lies
that you’re telling yourself be?

01:12:33.560 --> 01:12:37.300
And the answer to that is just exactly the
size that will make you happy.

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:40.810
Well that’s not helpful, because if you’re
going to have a theory, let’s hear about

01:12:40.810 --> 01:12:42.640
exactly how big they should be.

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:46.580
Well sometimes they should be this big and
other times they should be this big, but there’s

01:12:46.580 --> 01:12:48.980
no real way of telling a priori.

01:12:48.980 --> 01:12:53.300
Well so that’s not really very helpful either.

01:12:53.300 --> 01:12:57.130
And then there’s another idea underneath
that that’s even worse, which is reality

01:12:57.130 --> 01:12:59.500
is so awful that you can’t look at it.

01:12:59.500 --> 01:13:05.590
Well that’s nasty man, because that means
(and that’s what the positive illusion stuff

01:13:05.590 --> 01:13:09.900
meant, sort of like the terror management
theorists) if you got an unvarnished look

01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:13.480
at reality, it would just destroy you.

01:13:13.480 --> 01:13:20.040
Because you’re not strong enough to look
at reality in an unvarnished manner.

01:13:20.040 --> 01:13:24.390
Well, you can imagine why people would believe
that.

01:13:24.390 --> 01:13:30.180
You could think God yes, it’s pretty damn
awful.

01:13:30.180 --> 01:13:35.730
But then you think, well wait a minute.

01:13:35.730 --> 01:13:41.000
Is it really the case that everyone who’s
out there coping with the world is blind and

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:42.000
deluded?

01:13:42.000 --> 01:13:45.300
Or maybe is it that the people who are not
coping with the world very well are blind

01:13:45.300 --> 01:13:46.300
and deluded?

01:13:46.300 --> 01:13:47.840
I mean, ask yourself that.

01:13:47.840 --> 01:13:52.780
You’ve seen people in your life that you
admire, I presume, and certainly you’ve

01:13:52.780 --> 01:13:54.640
seen people you don’t admire.

01:13:54.640 --> 01:13:57.790
Well, so let’s take the ones you don’t
admire.

01:13:57.790 --> 01:14:02.270
Well, how about blind and deluded, does that
partially describe them?

01:14:02.270 --> 01:14:05.020
It would be blind, deluded, bitter, resentful,
and mean.

01:14:05.020 --> 01:14:08.380
That sort of covers the territory.

01:14:08.380 --> 01:14:10.240
And then the people you admire?

01:14:10.240 --> 01:14:13.810
Well, they’re strong people, generally.

01:14:13.810 --> 01:14:18.210
They can do things that are really, ridiculously
difficult, like have a baby, and raise it

01:14:18.210 --> 01:14:19.750
without destroying it.

01:14:19.750 --> 01:14:23.970
Or work in a palliative care ward or be a
nurse in a cancer ward for children.

01:14:23.970 --> 01:14:27.500
I mean, there’s a lot of awful things.

01:14:27.500 --> 01:14:29.060
I talked to this guy the other day.

01:14:29.060 --> 01:14:31.270
I just met him sort of randomly.

01:14:31.270 --> 01:14:32.780
And he had had a motorcycle accident.

01:14:32.780 --> 01:14:34.220
And it was a bad one.

01:14:34.220 --> 01:14:38.370
He rolled like 200 feet at 60 miles an hour
and he broke seven ribs and you know, had

01:14:38.370 --> 01:14:40.180
a brain hemorrhage.

01:14:40.180 --> 01:14:41.310
Like it really took it out of him.

01:14:41.310 --> 01:14:42.820
He was a good athlete before that.

01:14:42.820 --> 01:14:44.640
And so it probably aged him twenty years.

01:14:44.640 --> 01:14:47.450
And he’s still recovering.

01:14:47.450 --> 01:14:51.580
And he was telling me, he works for a power
company, and so he’s climbing up these power

01:14:51.580 --> 01:14:53.430
poles, you know, because that’s part of
his job.

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:55.540
Well now he’s pretty screwed up.

01:14:55.540 --> 01:14:58.480
He’s in his mid-fifties, he’s still climbing
up power poles.

01:14:58.480 --> 01:15:01.610
And he was working with a guy who had Parkinson’s
disease.

01:15:01.610 --> 01:15:05.452
And so the guy with the Parkinson’s disease
is at the top of the power pole and he’s

01:15:05.452 --> 01:15:06.452
doing his job.

01:15:06.452 --> 01:15:09.410
And he’s getting shaky, so they’re afraid
things are going to fall, and then this guy

01:15:09.410 --> 01:15:10.940
comes up behind him to help him.

01:15:10.940 --> 01:15:13.020
He’s only really got one arm that works.

01:15:13.020 --> 01:15:15.350
It’s like, Jesus, that’s tough.

01:15:15.350 --> 01:15:17.710
Those people are tough, man.

01:15:17.710 --> 01:15:19.580
You know, because Parkinson’s?

01:15:19.580 --> 01:15:20.650
That sucks.

01:15:20.650 --> 01:15:25.010
And well, so does having half your arm more
or less destroyed by a motorcycle accident

01:15:25.010 --> 01:15:27.980
and not staying at home hiding under the bed.

01:15:27.980 --> 01:15:31.540
You know, they’re out there still making
sure the lights are on.

01:15:31.540 --> 01:15:37.370
Well, that’s a pretty unvarnished confrontation
with reality as far as I’m concerned.

01:15:37.370 --> 01:15:43.850
You know, so I could say… alright, so here’s
another story.

01:15:43.850 --> 01:15:50.550
So, I’ve probably told you a little bit
about this before.

01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:56.070
When the behaviourists first proposed that
you could use exposure to treat anxiety, the

01:15:56.070 --> 01:16:00.400
psychoanalysts were all skeptical about that,
because they thought, well you’re just treating

01:16:00.400 --> 01:16:01.560
the symptom.

01:16:01.560 --> 01:16:07.820
They usually thought of a phobia as the manifestation,
the visible manifestation of something that

01:16:07.820 --> 01:16:10.820
lurked more deeply underground.

01:16:10.820 --> 01:16:13.160
And there’s real truth in that.

01:16:13.160 --> 01:16:19.060
Like, whatever a mouse is to someone that’s
mouse-phobic isn’t what a mouse is to you.

01:16:19.060 --> 01:16:22.461
The mouse is a bunch of other things, because
otherwise they wouldn’t be afraid of it.

01:16:22.461 --> 01:16:23.810
And so all those…

01:16:23.810 --> 01:16:27.560
you could say the mouse is an exemplar of
the category of all things that they’re

01:16:27.560 --> 01:16:28.560
afraid of.

01:16:28.560 --> 01:16:31.340
And that’s a good way of thinking what that
might mean symbolically.

01:16:31.340 --> 01:16:35.890
And so I had one client who was afraid of
mice, and she thought one would run up her

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:36.890
leg.

01:16:36.890 --> 01:16:37.890
And she was also afraid of being raped.

01:16:37.890 --> 01:16:39.680
And those two things were…

01:16:39.680 --> 01:16:45.890
there was a metaphorical union between them,
even though they’re obviously not exactly

01:16:45.890 --> 01:16:46.890
the same.

01:16:46.890 --> 01:16:50.510
So the psychoanalysts, they’re making their
point, you know.

01:16:50.510 --> 01:16:54.460
But it turned out that… so the original
behavioural theory was you can’t be anxious

01:16:54.460 --> 01:16:56.990
and calm at the same time.

01:16:56.990 --> 01:16:59.982
So what you do first, if you’re going to
teach someone to not be afraid of a mouse,

01:16:59.982 --> 01:17:02.130
is teach them how not to be afraid.

01:17:02.130 --> 01:17:06.820
So you put them in a chair and you do a relaxation
exercise with them, and then you show them

01:17:06.820 --> 01:17:08.190
a picture of a mouse.

01:17:08.190 --> 01:17:10.110
And you say, just breathe, you know, calm
down.

01:17:10.110 --> 01:17:11.290
And so they calm down.

01:17:11.290 --> 01:17:15.340
And then sooner or later they can look at
the pictures of mice by themselves, and then

01:17:15.340 --> 01:17:20.630
maybe you could throw a stuffed mouse at them,
and then maybe they could walk by a pet store

01:17:20.630 --> 01:17:24.720
and look at a mouse, and so on and so forth,
till you get them holding rats.

01:17:24.720 --> 01:17:27.530
And so, you know, that works.

01:17:27.530 --> 01:17:30.030
But it works even if you don’t relax.

01:17:30.030 --> 01:17:33.130
In fact it works just as well.

01:17:33.130 --> 01:17:38.830
So the old behavioural idea that it was counter-conditioning
was wrong, just like the idea that the reason

01:17:38.830 --> 01:17:40.830
that you were afraid of a rat was because
of conditioning.

01:17:40.830 --> 01:17:44.850
It’s like, of course you’re afraid of
a rat, it’s a rat.

01:17:44.850 --> 01:17:50.460
Just like you’re afraid of snakes, you know,
to a lesser or greater degree.

01:17:50.460 --> 01:17:53.740
And people actually become more afraid of
snakes as they get older, interestingly enough.

01:17:53.740 --> 01:17:57.790
Which is not what you really expect, right,
unless it was a biological function.

01:17:57.790 --> 01:18:03.600
So anyways, all you have to do is show people
the thing they’re afraid of.

01:18:03.600 --> 01:18:05.310
And what do they learn?

01:18:05.310 --> 01:18:07.160
They don’t learn that the thing isn’t
frightening.

01:18:07.160 --> 01:18:08.420
They learn that they’re tough.

01:18:08.420 --> 01:18:16.060
And so one of the things… and that’s why
it generalises, which is… see the psychoanalysts

01:18:16.060 --> 01:18:18.720
predicted that what would happen is, well,
you know, yeah, yeah, you’re not afraid

01:18:18.720 --> 01:18:23.740
of a mouse anymore, but you’re twice as
afraid of being raped in your cabin when you’re

01:18:23.740 --> 01:18:24.740
up there alone.

01:18:24.740 --> 01:18:26.850
It’s like no, that isn’t what happens.

01:18:26.850 --> 01:18:30.360
You’re not afraid of the mouse anymore and
you’re half as afraid of being alone up

01:18:30.360 --> 01:18:31.550
in your cabin.

01:18:31.550 --> 01:18:32.550
It generalises.

01:18:32.550 --> 01:18:36.640
And so if you take someone who’s agoraphobic
and they’re in their mid-forties, and somewhat

01:18:36.640 --> 01:18:40.450
dependent, and you treat them for agoraphobia,
then they’ll go home and give their husband

01:18:40.450 --> 01:18:43.700
hell (because they’re usually women), and
tell them all the things that they’ve been

01:18:43.700 --> 01:18:46.470
wanting to tell them for twenty years but
have been too afraid to because they thought

01:18:46.470 --> 01:18:47.470
he might leave.

01:18:47.470 --> 01:18:51.300
And then they were afraid that he’d leave
and they’d fall apart and all of that.

01:18:51.300 --> 01:18:52.850
So they get…

01:18:52.850 --> 01:18:56.080
you teach them that they don’t have to run
out of a subway and all of a sudden they get

01:18:56.080 --> 01:18:57.080
more confrontational.

01:18:57.080 --> 01:19:00.200
And the reason for that is because they get
more courageous.

01:19:00.200 --> 01:19:04.930
And the reason for that is they go do something
difficult and they watch themselves and hey,

01:19:04.930 --> 01:19:06.330
they could do it.

01:19:06.330 --> 01:19:11.460
And so one of the things you might ask about
yourself is, how do you know what you have

01:19:11.460 --> 01:19:13.700
to run away from?

01:19:13.700 --> 01:19:17.330
You know, you might be so damn tough you just
can’t bloody well imagine it, and you’re

01:19:17.330 --> 01:19:18.860
just sort of sheltered.

01:19:18.860 --> 01:19:25.560
Now, look, you’re a lot less sheltered than
you were when you were three, I presume.

01:19:25.560 --> 01:19:27.670
How many of you live on your own?

01:19:27.670 --> 01:19:31.310
Okay, now, that was a transition, I presume.

01:19:31.310 --> 01:19:35.000
How many of you were depressed and anxious
their first year of university?

01:19:35.000 --> 01:19:37.990
Okay, how many of you weren’t?

01:19:37.990 --> 01:19:42.080
Okay, so it’s about 50%.

01:19:42.080 --> 01:19:43.080
Well that’s about right.

01:19:43.080 --> 01:19:47.020
About a third of first-year UofT meet clinical
criteria for depression according to the Beck

01:19:47.020 --> 01:19:48.020
Depression Inventory.

01:19:48.020 --> 01:19:49.020
Well why?

01:19:49.020 --> 01:19:50.040
Well it’s hard.

01:19:50.040 --> 01:19:53.590
You know, you leave your family, you’re
kind of protected there, you go out, you have

01:19:53.590 --> 01:19:57.030
to figure out all the things you have to do
to keep yourself alive and then you have to

01:19:57.030 --> 01:19:59.880
go to university and they’re going to give
you an impossible amount of work that no one

01:19:59.880 --> 01:20:02.190
could ever possibly master.

01:20:02.190 --> 01:20:03.190
So it’s daunting.

01:20:03.190 --> 01:20:06.300
And then you’re surrounded by people who
are smarter than the people you’re used

01:20:06.300 --> 01:20:07.340
to being surrounded by.

01:20:07.340 --> 01:20:09.190
So that means that you’re not as smart as
you thought you were.

01:20:09.190 --> 01:20:13.060
And then you meet people now and then who
are way smarter than you and way harder-working

01:20:13.060 --> 01:20:14.960
and way better-looking and all of those things.

01:20:14.960 --> 01:20:17.960
So you know, it’s depressing.

01:20:17.960 --> 01:20:23.940
But by the same token, how many of you feel
less armed to take on the world than you did

01:20:23.940 --> 01:20:25.080
five years ago?

01:20:25.080 --> 01:20:29.110
You’re not going to admit it, probably,
but I presume that that’s a very small number.

01:20:29.110 --> 01:20:31.220
How many of you feel more prepared?

01:20:31.220 --> 01:20:34.110
Okay, okay, so that’s pretty cool.

01:20:34.110 --> 01:20:37.390
So what that means is that you went out there
and did a whole bunch of difficult things.

01:20:37.390 --> 01:20:38.720
And some of those things…

01:20:38.720 --> 01:20:42.010
you’ve been reading each other’s autobiographies
right?

01:20:42.010 --> 01:20:43.580
How’s that going?

01:20:43.580 --> 01:20:45.680
Has it been interesting?

01:20:45.680 --> 01:20:48.310
Yeah, has it been worthwhile?

01:20:48.310 --> 01:20:49.540
Anybody think it isn’t worthwhile?

01:20:49.540 --> 01:20:54.230
I really want to know, you know, because it’s
kind of an experiment, in some sense, to see

01:20:54.230 --> 01:20:55.230
how this is working.

01:20:55.230 --> 01:20:56.230
What’s good about it?

01:20:56.230 --> 01:21:02.550
[Student: That you can get to know a stranger
on the deepest level possible.]

01:21:02.550 --> 01:21:05.810
So why do you like that?

01:21:05.810 --> 01:21:10.170
[Student: Because it makes the class feel
more…]

01:21:10.170 --> 01:21:14.360
Yeah you know I’m thinking about working
with a company in Edmonton that is setting

01:21:14.360 --> 01:21:20.270
that up as a way of increasing trust among
people who work together in the same company.

01:21:20.270 --> 01:21:24.210
They’re not really sharing their deepest
secrets, but what they will be doing is having

01:21:24.210 --> 01:21:28.070
structured conversations where they find out
a little bit about each other.

01:21:28.070 --> 01:21:32.530
You know, and it’s sort of… it seems like
to have a relationship with someone you have

01:21:32.530 --> 01:21:34.540
to get to know their hierarchical structure,
right?

01:21:34.540 --> 01:21:39.970
And it’s just like… like the tiniest things
while that person sits at the desk and types,

01:21:39.970 --> 01:21:42.280
well, it’s a shallow relationship.

01:21:42.280 --> 01:21:46.690
And if it’s a deep relationship you know
much more about the whole structure, right?

01:21:46.690 --> 01:21:50.090
Which is I think why the metaphor of depth
works so well.

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:52.691
Okay, so you feel like it catalysed the classroom
a little bit more, is that right?

01:21:52.691 --> 01:21:59.890
[Student: Yeah, and isn’t it like you fall
in love with someone…

01:21:59.890 --> 01:22:08.050
this isn’t about falling in love, but you
fall in love with someone when they’re vulnerable?]

01:22:08.050 --> 01:22:11.410
Yeah so that’s interesting too eh about
the whole vulnerability issue.

01:22:11.410 --> 01:22:17.430
Yeah it’s like, if you’re forming a relationship
with someone what you do is you basically

01:22:17.430 --> 01:22:20.650
mutually open up your shells you know.

01:22:20.650 --> 01:22:24.250
Or basically you kind of go like this [opens
and closes hands].

01:22:24.250 --> 01:22:27.190
And if it has to close permanently well then
you go.

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:31.790
Yeah well that’s another interesting thing
too because what it implies is that unless,

01:22:31.790 --> 01:22:37.870
you know, you voluntarily expose your vulnerabilities
you’ll never find a partner, or they won’t

01:22:37.870 --> 01:22:40.380
be a real partner because they don’t know
who the hell you are.

01:22:40.380 --> 01:22:41.810
And then you’re weak because there’s only
one of you.

01:22:41.810 --> 01:22:42.810
Yeah?

01:22:42.810 --> 01:22:48.270
[Student: And the beauty in that is that it’s
completely anonymous too.]

01:22:48.270 --> 01:22:49.860
Yeah and so why do you like that?

01:22:49.860 --> 01:22:57.210
[Student: Because I can share my deepest secrets
but not feel like anyone will…]

01:22:57.210 --> 01:23:01.710
You know there’s some evidence that people
like computer-guided psychotherapy.

01:23:01.710 --> 01:23:03.871
For exactly that reason.

01:23:03.871 --> 01:23:07.230
You can be completely honest with the computer
because it doesn’t care.

01:23:07.230 --> 01:23:08.230
Not yet.

01:23:08.230 --> 01:23:13.160
It will soon but, but it probably won’t
judge you any more harshly than you set it

01:23:13.160 --> 01:23:14.160
to.

01:23:14.160 --> 01:23:17.500
It’s like, love and affection today.

01:23:17.500 --> 01:23:18.500
[Laughter].

01:23:18.500 --> 01:23:20.570
Yeah, well that won’t be very far away.

01:23:20.570 --> 01:23:23.680
Okay so what do other people have to say about
the autobiography?

01:23:23.680 --> 01:23:24.680
Yeah?

01:23:24.680 --> 01:23:25.680
[Student: I found it really interesting because
I never personally know so deeply what another

01:23:25.680 --> 01:23:26.680
person thinks, because we don’t share that
much…

01:23:26.680 --> 01:23:40.060
we talk about the beginning of childhood up
to this point, we never really have that conversation

01:23:40.060 --> 01:23:42.730
with anyone else.]

01:23:42.730 --> 01:23:44.160
So you like that too.

01:23:44.160 --> 01:23:46.480
[Student: Yeah I like it a lot.]

01:23:46.480 --> 01:23:47.480
Why?

01:23:47.480 --> 01:23:48.480
What was good about it for you?

01:23:48.480 --> 01:23:49.640
[Student: Usually as we move through stages
in our life we only interact with our age,

01:23:49.640 --> 01:23:51.840
we never see them holistically.

01:23:51.840 --> 01:24:01.420
But watching them grow, it’s kind of like,
very strange, because we don’t see people

01:24:01.420 --> 01:24:02.700
that way.]

01:24:02.700 --> 01:24:06.070
Right, well you’re seeing more of this eh?

01:24:06.070 --> 01:24:07.159
[Points to Ouroboros image].

01:24:07.159 --> 01:24:11.880
Because the person actually extends across
time, and you’re right, all you see is a

01:24:11.880 --> 01:24:12.880
slice.

01:24:12.880 --> 01:24:17.320
Well they’re not a slice, they’re a whole
damn thing, this thing that grows across time.

01:24:17.320 --> 01:24:21.190
Okay, who else has something to say that’s
interesting?

01:24:21.190 --> 01:24:22.190
[Laughter].

01:24:22.190 --> 01:24:26.280
Haha, half the people don’t want to talk
now.

01:24:26.280 --> 01:24:31.060
So, anybody else?
[Student: After doing that and writing so

01:24:31.060 --> 01:24:33.930
many things about myself, I was actually talking
about the assignment with a couple of my friends,

01:24:33.930 --> 01:24:40.040
and I really thought, I’d actually be okay
if the people who read my assignment identified

01:24:40.040 --> 01:24:45.090
who I was and like, even engaged in conversation
with me.

01:24:45.090 --> 01:24:53.210
And it really inspired me to be more honest
about sharing myself in my existing relationships.]

01:24:53.210 --> 01:24:57.940
So why did you conclude that it would be okay
if someone identified you?

01:24:57.940 --> 01:25:02.490
[Student: Because I felt a desire to be seen
more.

01:25:02.490 --> 01:25:04.470
And it was kind of…

01:25:04.470 --> 01:25:08.250
I felt more with myself when I was disclosing
more information about myself.

01:25:08.250 --> 01:25:15.470
And so it made me want to be more with myself
in my existing relationships.]

01:25:15.470 --> 01:25:17.280
Okay, that was a very good observation.

01:25:17.280 --> 01:25:21.240
I’m going to say a couple of things about
that and you tell me if you think they’re

01:25:21.240 --> 01:25:22.240
accurate.

01:25:22.240 --> 01:25:27.230
Okay so the first thing is, it’s an act
of courage to, haha, expose yourself to someone…

01:25:27.230 --> 01:25:30.200
no we’re not going to say that precisely.

01:25:30.200 --> 01:25:36.120
It’s an act of courage to be truthful with
someone.

01:25:36.120 --> 01:25:40.790
Because they’re actually interacting with
the actual you.

01:25:40.790 --> 01:25:41.910
So there’s no façade.

01:25:41.910 --> 01:25:43.180
So that’s one thing.

01:25:43.180 --> 01:25:45.150
So you get more courageous.

01:25:45.150 --> 01:25:50.060
But then the next thing might be…

01:25:50.060 --> 01:25:55.190
I would say that you have a more sympathetic
relationship with yourself.

01:25:55.190 --> 01:25:58.010
Because let’s say you have this stupid memory
about something dumb that you did when you

01:25:58.010 --> 01:25:59.010
were 13.

01:25:59.010 --> 01:26:00.740
And you’re still carrying that along.

01:26:00.740 --> 01:26:04.179
It’s like, Jesus, you were 13, what the
hell do you expect?

01:26:04.179 --> 01:26:07.590
13-year-olds are just dumb as sticks, they’re
going to do all sorts of stupid things.

01:26:07.590 --> 01:26:12.060
Their brains are all addled from hormones
and they’re trapped in those little educational

01:26:12.060 --> 01:26:13.060
prisons.

01:26:13.060 --> 01:26:15.590
So they’re going to do stupid things.

01:26:15.590 --> 01:26:19.000
But if you’re carrying that it’s like
it’s you that did that.

01:26:19.000 --> 01:26:22.130
It’s not some stupid 13-year-old that you
once were.

01:26:22.130 --> 01:26:27.630
And so then you can start to see yourself
in perspective, you know, as a 13-year-old

01:26:27.630 --> 01:26:29.040
like other 13-year-olds.

01:26:29.040 --> 01:26:31.770
No better, but perhaps no worse.

01:26:31.770 --> 01:26:37.980
So you can get on your side, in some sense,
by walking through your story and seeing what

01:26:37.980 --> 01:26:39.380
the causal elements are.

01:26:39.380 --> 01:26:40.950
Does that seem reasonable?

01:26:40.950 --> 01:26:44.890
Okay, okay, yeah, well so that’s a good
thing.

01:26:44.890 --> 01:26:45.920
[Student: I think…

01:26:45.920 --> 01:26:52.620
I really liked it, again, because you really
get to know other people and I really liked

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:53.620
that.

01:26:53.620 --> 01:26:59.850
I think that it was also interesting that
other people not necessarily knew more about

01:26:59.850 --> 01:27:05.110
you than you did, but they contemplated the
information that you gave them… like I know

01:27:05.110 --> 01:27:08.220
that someone looked at something that I’d
written in a completely different light than

01:27:08.220 --> 01:27:10.220
I did, and I thought that was so interesting.

01:27:10.220 --> 01:27:15.350
Just like, the idea of kind of looking at
it a little bit more from the outside, and

01:27:15.350 --> 01:27:17.140
getting a different perspective on it.]

01:27:17.140 --> 01:27:23.460
Right, well that’s… what’s the famous
poem on the best gift we can possibly be given

01:27:23.460 --> 01:27:25.280
is to see ourselves as others see us?

01:27:25.280 --> 01:27:29.710
I think it’s Robbie Burns and I just mangled
the hell out of it.

01:27:29.710 --> 01:27:33.830
But yeah, you do get that opportunity, and
it’s weird.

01:27:33.830 --> 01:27:36.670
And this is something the positive illusion
people never think about either.

01:27:36.670 --> 01:27:41.890
It’s that sometimes people think they’re
better than they are, like objectively, whatever

01:27:41.890 --> 01:27:42.890
that means.

01:27:42.890 --> 01:27:46.860
But lots of times the opposite is true, and
I think it’s often it’s true even more

01:27:46.860 --> 01:27:47.860
often.

01:27:47.860 --> 01:27:49.280
It’s like, people think they’re a lot
worse than they are.

01:27:49.280 --> 01:27:54.980
No one’s as anxious as me or as concerned
about X or is as confused about this or as

01:27:54.980 --> 01:27:59.420
socially awkward, or is not funny, or you
know, everybody’s got their paranoia about

01:27:59.420 --> 01:28:04.340
this part of them that’s the worst that
anyone could possibly be in the entire world

01:28:04.340 --> 01:28:08.580
and it’s almost never true.

01:28:08.580 --> 01:28:10.870
Other people are a lot more like that than
you think.

01:28:10.870 --> 01:28:14.720
And they’re running around trying to hide
it madly as well.

01:28:14.720 --> 01:28:16.830
So that’s a good thing.

01:28:16.830 --> 01:28:20.290
Well you know, you’re also allowing yourself…
in some sense you’re allowing yourself up

01:28:20.290 --> 01:28:21.290
into the light.

01:28:21.290 --> 01:28:23.260
You go through your life and you think, well
I can allow other people…

